export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"In what way does the Indian Parliament exercise control over the administration?","A":"Through Consultative Committees of various ministries","B":"Through Parliamentary Committees","C":"By making the administrators send periodic reports","D":"By compelling the executive to issue writs","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Parliamentary Committees","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Parliament exercises detailed scrutiny of administration through its Parliamentary Committees.\nThese committees examine government expenditure, policies, administration and legislative matters.\nThey help Parliament maintain accountability over the executive.","revisionPoints":"Parliament controls the executive through:\nQuestions and debates\nMotions\nParliamentary Committees\nBudgetary control\nImportant committees include:\nPublic Accounts Committee\nEstimates Committee\nCommittee on Public Undertakings\nParliamentary committees are important instruments of legislative oversight."},
{"no":2,"text":"Who among the following organised the famous Chittagong Armoury Raid?","A":"Laxmi Sehgal","B":"Surya Sen","C":"Batukeshwar Datta","D":"J. M. Sengupta","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Chittagong Armoury Raid","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Surya Sen, popularly known as Masterda, organised the Chittagong Armoury Raid.\nThe raid took place in 1930 in Bengal.\nIts objective was to seize arms and challenge British authority.","revisionPoints":"Chittagong Armoury Raid → 1930.\nLeader → Surya Sen.\nAssociated with revolutionary nationalism in Bengal.\nSurya Sen was later captured and executed by the British.\nImportant associated revolutionary: Pritilata Waddedar."},
{"no":3,"text":"Consider the following decadal Census data:\nYear\nPopulation (million)\n1961\n10.7\n1971\n14.3\n1981\n16.2\n1991\n18.9\nThe above data refer to which one of the following Population by Religion groups?","A":"Sikhs","B":"Jains","C":"Christians","D":"Buddhists","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Census","tags":["Religious Demographics","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The population figures given in the question correspond to the Christian population according to the source.\nThe question tests interpretation of historical Census data.\nReligious demographic data can be used to compare population trends across communities.","revisionPoints":"Indian Census provides demographic data by:\nReligion\nLanguage\nPopulation\nLiteracy\nMigration\nCensus-based questions often require recognising numerical trends.\nAlways pay attention to the census year and units."},
{"no":4,"text":"Which of the following committees examined and suggested Financial Sector Reforms?","A":"Abid Hussain Committee","B":"Bhagwati Committee","C":"Chelliah Committee","D":"Narasimham Committee","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Sector Reforms","tags":["Narasimham Committee","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Narasimham Committee examined reforms in India's financial and banking sector.\nIts recommendations focused on improving:\nBanking efficiency\nFinancial discipline\nCapital adequacy\nReduction of non-performing assets\nIt played an important role in India's financial-sector reform process.","revisionPoints":"Narasimham Committee → Banking and financial-sector reforms.\nTwo major reports are associated with 1991 and 1998.\nImportant themes:\nBanking autonomy\nPrudential norms\nCapital adequacy\nReduction of NPAs\nChelliah Committee → Tax reforms."},
{"no":5,"text":"In which one of the following cities is the Lingaraja Temple located?","A":"Bhubaneswar","B":"Bijapur","C":"Kolkata","D":"Shravanabelagola","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Temple Architecture","tags":["Lingaraja Temple","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Lingaraja Temple is located in Bhubaneswar, Odisha.\nIt is one of the major temples of the Kalinga architectural tradition.\nThe temple is primarily dedicated to Lord Shiva.","revisionPoints":"Lingaraja Temple → Bhubaneswar, Odisha.\nImportant example of Kalinga architecture.\nBhubaneswar is popularly associated with numerous ancient temples.\nOdisha temple architecture is broadly associated with the Nagara tradition."},
{"no":6,"text":"A London branch of the All-India Muslim League was established in 1908 under the presidency of:","A":"Agha Khan","B":"Ameer Ali","C":"Liaquat Ali Khan","D":"M. A. Jinnah","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Muslim League","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The London branch of the All-India Muslim League was established under Ameer Ali.\nThe organisation was established in the context of increasing political mobilisation among Indian Muslims.\nAmeer Ali was an important Muslim intellectual and political figure.","revisionPoints":"All-India Muslim League → founded in 1906.\nFounding session → Dacca (Dhaka).\nLondon branch → associated with Ameer Ali.\nMuslim League became an important political organisation in late colonial India."},
{"no":7,"text":"The map given in the original paper shows the Union Territory of:","A":"Chandigarh","B":"Daman and Diu","C":"Dadra and Nagar Haveli","D":"Pondicherry","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Union Territories","tags":["Dadra and Nagar Haveli","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies the mapped territory as Dadra and Nagar Haveli.\nIt was a Union Territory of India during the period of the examination.\nThe question is based on geographical identification from the original map.","revisionPoints":"Dadra and Nagar Haveli → western India.\nLocated between Gujarat and Maharashtra.\nDadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu were later merged into one Union Territory.\nMap-based questions require attention to location and neighbouring states."},
{"no":8,"text":"Identify the correct order of the processes of soil erosion:","A":"Splash erosion → Sheet erosion → Rill erosion → Gully erosion","B":"Sheet erosion → Splash erosion → Gully erosion → Rill erosion","C":"Rill erosion → Gully erosion → Sheet erosion → Splash erosion","D":"Gully erosion → Rill erosion → Sheet erosion → Splash erosion","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Soil Erosion","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Splash erosion is caused by the impact of raindrops on soil.\nSheet erosion removes a thin, relatively uniform layer of soil.\nRill erosion creates small channels.\nGully erosion develops when channels become deeper and larger.","revisionPoints":"Splash → initial raindrop impact.\nSheet → removal of thin soil layer.\nRill → small channels.\nGully → large/deep channels.\nVegetation cover helps reduce soil erosion."},
{"no":9,"text":"The new series of Wholesale Price Index (WPI) released by the Government of India was with reference to the base prices of:","A":"1981–82","B":"1990–91","C":"1993–94","D":"1994–95","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Inflation","tags":["Wholesale Price Index","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies 1993–94 as the base year.\nA base year provides the reference against which changes in prices are measured.\nWPI is used to track wholesale-level price movements.","revisionPoints":"WPI measures price changes at the wholesale level.\nBase year = reference period for constructing an index.\nWPI covers broad commodity groups.\nDo not confuse WPI with Consumer Price Index (CPI)."},
{"no":10,"text":"The chess player Alexei Shirov represents:","A":"Albania","B":"Kazakhstan","C":"Russia","D":"Spain","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Chess","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies Spain as Shirov's representation.\nAlexei Shirov is a prominent chess grandmaster.\nThe question tests international sports/current-affairs knowledge of the period.","revisionPoints":"Alexei Shirov → chess Grandmaster.\nKnown for an aggressive and tactical playing style.\nChess-related questions can appear under sports current affairs.\nCountry representation may differ from a player's country of birth or origin."},
{"no":11,"text":"Consider the following organisations:\nAtomic Minerals Directorate for Research and Exploration\nHeavy Water Board\nIndian Rare Earths Limited\nUranium Corporation of India\nWhich of these is/are under the Department of Atomic Energy?","A":"I only","B":"I and IV","C":"II, III and IV","D":"I, II, III and IV","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Energy","tags":["Department of Atomic Energy","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source, all four organisations are under the administrative framework of the Department of Atomic Energy.\nThey are associated with different parts of India's nuclear-energy ecosystem.\nTheir functions include exploration, processing and management of nuclear-related materials and resources.","revisionPoints":"Department of Atomic Energy → India's nuclear-energy administration.\nAtomic Minerals Directorate → exploration of atomic minerals.\nHeavy Water Board → heavy-water production.\nUranium Corporation of India → uranium mining/processing.\nIndian Rare Earths → minerals and rare-earth-related activities."},
{"no":12,"text":"Who among the following was the President of the All-India States' Peoples' Conference in 1939?","A":"Jayaprakash Narayan","B":"Jawaharlal Nehru","C":"Sheikh Abdullah","D":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["States' Peoples' Conference","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Jawaharlal Nehru served as President of the All-India States' Peoples' Conference in 1939.\nThe organisation was associated with political movements in the princely states.\nIt supported the demand for greater political rights and responsible government in princely territories.","revisionPoints":"All-India States' Peoples' Conference → movements in princely states.\nJawaharlal Nehru → President in 1939.\nImportant in understanding the political integration of princely states.\nPrincely-state movements became increasingly connected with the broader national movement."},
{"no":13,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding minorities in India:\nThe Government of India has notified five communities — Muslims, Sikhs, Christians, Buddhists and Zoroastrians — as minorities.\nThe National Commission for Minorities was given statutory status in 1993.\nThe smallest religious minority in India are the Zoroastrians.\nThe Constitution of India recognises and protects religious and linguistic minorities.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"II and III","B":"I and IV","C":"II, III and IV","D":"I, II and IV","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Minority Rights","tags":["National Commission for Minorities","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source: Five communities were notified as minorities at the time.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The National Commission for Minorities received statutory status in 1993.\nStatement 3 — Not accepted by the source: The statement about the smallest religious minority is not included in the correct combination.\nStatement 4 — Correct: The Constitution provides protection for religious and linguistic minorities.","revisionPoints":"Constitutional minority protections are particularly associated with Articles 29 and 30.\nArticle 29 → protection of interests of sections having distinct language, script or culture.\nArticle 30 → minorities' right to establish and administer educational institutions.\nNational Commission for Minorities → statutory body from 1993."},
{"no":14,"text":"The following temperature and rainfall data are given for a meteorological station:\nAverage temperature: 12.8°C\nAverage rainfall: 54.9 cm per annum\nRainfall is concentrated mainly during the cooler months.\nIdentify the region having this climatic pattern:","A":"Mediterranean region","B":"Monsoon region","C":"Steppe region","D":"N.W. European region","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Mediterranean Climate","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The rainfall pattern is concentrated mainly in the winter months.\nSummers are comparatively dry.\nThis seasonal pattern is characteristic of the Mediterranean climate.\nMediterranean climates generally have hot/dry summers and mild/wet winters.","revisionPoints":"Mediterranean climate:\nDry summer\nWet winter\nWinter rainfall\nFound around the Mediterranean Sea and in several other subtropical regions.\nImportant examples outside the Mediterranean:\nCalifornia\nCentral Chile\nCape region of South Africa\nSouth-western Australia"},
{"no":15,"text":"The theme of the World Development Report, 2001 was:","A":"From Plan to Market","B":"Knowledge for Development","C":"Attacking Poverty","D":"The State in the Changing World","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["World Development Report","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The World Development Report 2001 focused on poverty reduction.\nIt examined strategies for improving the lives of poor populations.\nMajor dimensions included opportunities, empowerment and security.","revisionPoints":"World Development Report → published by the World Bank.\n2001 theme → Attacking Poverty.\nPoverty is multidimensional and includes more than income deprivation.\nImportant dimensions include:\nOpportunity\nEmpowerment\nSecurity"},
{"no":16,"text":"In which one of the following areas does the State Government NOT have control over its local bodies?","A":"Citizens' grievances","B":"Financial matters","C":"Legislation","D":"Personnel matters","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Government","tags":["Local Self Government","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies personnel matters as the area where the State Government does not exercise the stated form of control over local bodies.\nLocal self-government involves decentralisation of administrative functions.\nThe constitutional framework seeks to provide local bodies with a degree of autonomy.","revisionPoints":"73rd Constitutional Amendment → Panchayati Raj institutions.\n74th Constitutional Amendment → Urban local bodies.\nLocal bodies deal with local development and civic administration.\nState legislation remains important in determining the detailed functioning of local governments."},
{"no":17,"text":"Who among the following Englishmen first translated the Bhagavad-Gita into English?","A":"William Jones","B":"Charles Wilkins","C":"Alexander Cunningham","D":"John Marshall","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Colonial History","tags":["Orientalism","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Charles Wilkins produced the first English translation of the Bhagavad-Gita.\nHis translation was published in the 18th century.\nWilkins was an important early European scholar of Sanskrit.","revisionPoints":"Charles Wilkins → early Sanskrit scholar and translator.\nBhagavad-Gita → part of the Mahabharata.\nOrientalist scholarship contributed to European study of Indian languages and texts.\nWilliam Jones was another major figure in the study of Indian languages and culture."},
{"no":18,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the High Courts in India:\nThere are eighteen High Courts in the country.\nThree of them have jurisdiction over more than one State.\nNo Union Territory has a High Court of its own.\nJudges of the High Court hold office till the age of 62.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"I, II and IV","B":"II and III","C":"I and IV","D":"IV only","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["High Courts","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source's 2001 context.\nStatement 2 — Correct according to the source: Certain High Courts exercised jurisdiction over more than one State.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The source does not accept the statement that no Union Territory has a High Court of its own.\nStatement 4 — Correct: High Court judges' retirement age was 62 years.","revisionPoints":"High Courts are constitutional courts under Part VI of the Constitution.\nHigh Court judges are appointed by the President of India.\nRetirement age of a High Court judge → 62 years.\nHigh Courts may have jurisdiction over more than one State/Union Territory."},
{"no":19,"text":"The term National Income represents:","A":"Gross National Product at market prices minus depreciation","B":"Gross National Product at market prices minus depreciation and net factor income from abroad","C":"Gross National Product at market prices minus depreciation and indirect taxes + subsidies","D":"Gross National Product at market prices minus net factor income from abroad","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"National Income","tags":["National Income","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies option (b).\nNational income is conceptually associated with Net National Product at factor cost.\nStarting from GNP at market prices:\nSubtract depreciation → Net National Product.\nAdjust for net indirect taxes to reach factor cost.\nThe wording in the supplied source is somewhat compressed/garbled, so the answer is retained according to the source key.","revisionPoints":"GDP → production within domestic territory.\nGNP → GDP + net factor income from abroad.\nNDP → GDP − depreciation.\nNNP → GNP − depreciation.\nNational income is generally expressed as NNP at factor cost."},
{"no":20,"text":"Match the following local winds with their regions:\nLocal Wind\nRegion\nI. Föhn\nA. Argentina\nII. Samun\nB. Kurdistan\nIII. Santa Ana\nC. California\nIV. Zonda\nD. Alps\nCodes:","A":"I-B, II-D, III-A, IV-C","B":"I-D, II-B, III-C, IV-A","C":"I-B, II-D, III-C, IV-A","D":"I-D, II-B, III-A, IV-C","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Local Winds","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Föhn → Alps: A warm, dry downslope wind.\nSamun → Kurdistan: A regional hot/dry wind.\nSanta Ana → California: A hot, dry wind affecting southern California.\nZonda → Argentina: A warm, dry downslope wind associated with the Andes.","revisionPoints":"Föhn → Alps.\nZonda → Argentina/Andes.\nSanta Ana → California.\nLocal winds are often classified according to:\nTemperature\nHumidity\nDirection\nRegional geography\nUPSC frequently asks local wind–region matching."},
{"no":21,"text":"The given map shows four towns of the Central Asian region marked as 1, 2, 3 and 4. Identify them:\nA. Bishkek\nB. Ashkhabad\nC. Tashkent\nD. Dushanbe","A":"A3, B1, C3, D4","B":"A3, B1, C4, D2","C":"A1, B3, C2, D4","D":"A1, B3, C4, D2","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Central Asia","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source's map-key:\nBishkek → 3\nAshkhabad → 1\nTashkent → 3\nDushanbe → 4\nTherefore, the correct code is (a).","revisionPoints":"Bishkek → Kyrgyzstan\nAshkhabad → Turkmenistan\nTashkent → Uzbekistan\nDushanbe → Tajikistan\nCentral Asia includes Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan."},
{"no":22,"text":"The Mekong-Ganga Cooperation Project is:","A":"An irrigation project involving India and Myanmar","B":"A joint tourism initiative of some Asian countries","C":"A hydroelectric power project involving India, Bangladesh and Myanmar","D":"A defence and security agreement of India with its eastern neighbours","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"India's Foreign Relations","tags":["Mekong Ganga Cooperation","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies it as a joint tourism initiative.\nThe Mekong-Ganga Cooperation brings together countries of the Mekong and Ganga regions.\nCooperation covers areas such as tourism, culture, education and transport.","revisionPoints":"Mekong-Ganga Cooperation → regional cooperation framework.\nIndia is connected with countries of Southeast Asia through this initiative.\nImportant areas include:\nTourism\nCulture\nEducation\nTransport\nIt reflects India's broader Look East orientation of that period."},
{"no":23,"text":"Match the following Constitutional Amendments with their contents:\nAmendment\nContent\nI. 69th Amendment, 1991\nA. Establishment of State-level Rent Tribunals\nII. 75th Amendment, 1994\nB. No reservations for SCs in Panchayats in Arunachal Pradesh\nIII. 80th Amendment, 2000\nC. Constitution of Panchayats in villages or other local levels\nIV. 83rd Amendment, 2000\nD. Accepting recommendations of the Tenth Finance Commission\nE. According NCT status to Delhi","A":"I-E, II-A, III-D, IV-B","B":"I-A, II-E, III-C, IV-D","C":"I-E, II-A, III-C, IV-D","D":"I-A, II-E, III-D, IV-B","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["Constitutional Amendments","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"69th Amendment → NCT status to Delhi.\n75th Amendment → State-level Rent Tribunals.\n80th Amendment → distribution of Union taxes/recommendations of the Finance Commission framework.\n83rd Amendment → special provision concerning Panchayats in Arunachal Pradesh.","revisionPoints":"69th Amendment → Delhi as National Capital Territory.\n75th Amendment → Rent Tribunals.\n80th Amendment → tax-sharing arrangement between Union and States.\nConstitutional amendment questions require matching the amendment number with its specific subject."},
{"no":24,"text":"The largest number of Buddhists is found in:","A":"Bihar","B":"Karnataka","C":"Maharashtra","D":"Uttar Pradesh","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Census","tags":["Buddhist Population","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source, Maharashtra had the largest Buddhist population among the options.\nMaharashtra has a significant Buddhist population, particularly associated with the Ambedkarite Buddhist movement.","revisionPoints":"Maharashtra has India's largest concentration of Buddhists in absolute numbers in the source's census context.\nDr. B. R. Ambedkar played a major role in the modern Buddhist movement in India.\nNagpur is historically important to the Dalit Buddhist movement."},
{"no":25,"text":"Who among the following was the Chairman of ISRO when INSAT-3B was launched?","A":"Anil Kakodkar","B":"A. P. J. Abdul Kalam","C":"K. Kasturirangan","D":"U. R. Rao","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["ISRO","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"K. Kasturirangan was Chairman of ISRO during the launch of INSAT-3B.\nINSAT-3B was a communication satellite.\nThe question tests knowledge of India's space programme and leadership during the period.","revisionPoints":"K. Kasturirangan → former ISRO Chairman.\nINSAT → Indian National Satellite system.\nINSAT satellites have supported:\nTelecommunications\nTelevision broadcasting\nMeteorological services\nSatellite-based services\nISRO is India's principal space agency."},
{"no":26,"text":"Assertion (A): The Battle of Khanua was certainly more decisive and significant than the First Battle of Panipat.\nReason (R): Rana Sanga, the Rajput hero, was certainly a more formidable adversary than Ibrahim Lodi.","A":"Both A and R are individually true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true, but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Battle of Khanua","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Assertion — False according to the source: The Battle of Khanua cannot simply be described as more decisive and significant than the First Battle of Panipat in the manner stated.\nReason — True according to the source: Rana Sanga was considered a formidable adversary.\nTherefore, the correct combination is A false, R true.","revisionPoints":"First Battle of Panipat → 1526.\nBabur defeated Ibrahim Lodi.\nBattle of Khanua → 1527.\nBabur defeated Rana Sanga.\nKhanua strengthened the position of the emerging Mughal power in North India."},
{"no":27,"text":"Assertion (A): Anticyclonic conditions are formed in winter season when atmospheric pressure is high and air temperatures are low.\nReason (R): Winter rainfall in Northern India causes development of anticyclonic conditions with low temperatures.","A":"Both A and R are individually true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true, but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Anticyclones","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Winter conditions can be associated with high pressure and low temperatures.\nReason — Incorrect: Winter rainfall is not the fundamental cause of the anticyclonic pressure conditions described.\nAnticyclones are associated with high-pressure systems and descending air.","revisionPoints":"Anticyclone → high-pressure system.\nAir generally descends in an anticyclone.\nNorthern Indian winter weather is influenced by:\nContinental high pressure\nWestern disturbances\nTemperature gradients\nDo not confuse anticyclones with cyclones, which are low-pressure systems."},
{"no":28,"text":"Assertion (A): Harshavardhana convened the Prayag Assembly.\nReason (R): He wanted to popularise only the Mahayana form of Buddhism.","A":"Both A and R are individually true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true, but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Harshavardhana","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source, Harshavardhana convened the Prayag Assembly.\nThe source associates the assembly with his support for Mahayana Buddhism.\nThe Prayag assembly was also associated with large-scale religious and charitable activities.","revisionPoints":"Harshavardhana → ruler of Thanesar and Kannauj.\nChinese traveller Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang) visited his kingdom.\nHarsha was a patron of learning and religion.\nPrayag Assembly → major religious gathering associated with Harsha."},
{"no":29,"text":"Assertion (A): There was an increase in industrial production during 1999–2000.\nReason (R): The period witnessed a stable exchange rate and improved business sentiments.","A":"Both A and R are individually true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true, but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industrial Growth","tags":["Industrial Production","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct according to the source: Industrial production increased during 1999–2000.\nReason — Correct: Stable exchange-rate conditions and improved business sentiment supported industrial activity.\nTherefore, the reason provides the explanation for the assertion.","revisionPoints":"Industrial production reflects activity in sectors such as:\nManufacturing\nMining\nElectricity\nBusiness confidence can influence investment and industrial output.\nExchange-rate stability can affect import costs, exports and business expectations."},
{"no":30,"text":"Assertion (A): India's software exports increased at an average growth rate of 50% since 1995–96.\nReason (R): Indian software companies were cost-effective and maintained international quality.","A":"Both A and R are individually true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true, but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"IT & Services","tags":["Software Exports","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct according to the source.\nReason — Correct according to the source: Cost competitiveness combined with international-quality services supported India's software-export growth.\nIndia's IT sector expanded rapidly during the 1990s.","revisionPoints":"India's software industry benefited from:\nSkilled workforce\nEnglish-language capabilities\nCost competitiveness\nGlobal outsourcing demand\nSoftware exports became an important source of foreign exchange earnings.\nThe 1990s marked rapid expansion of India's IT sector."},
{"no":31,"text":"Assertion (A): The ceiling on foreign exchange for a host of current-account transaction heads was lowered in the year 2000.\nReason (R): There was a fall in foreign currency assets also.","A":"Both A and R are individually true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true, but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["Foreign Exchange","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct according to the source.\nReason — Correct according to the source.\nHowever, the fall in foreign currency assets is not considered the correct explanation for the reduction in the stated foreign-exchange ceilings.\nHence, both are true but R does not explain A.","revisionPoints":"Current account transactions include:\nTrade in goods\nServices\nTransfers\nForeign exchange regulations can affect current-account transactions.\nForeign exchange reserves include foreign currency assets and other reserve components.\nAssertion–Reason questions require checking the causal relationship, not merely whether both statements are true."},
{"no":32,"text":"Assertion (A): During Neap Tides, the high tide is lower and the low tide is higher than usual.\nReason (R): Neap Tide, unlike Spring Tide, occurs on the New Moon instead of on the Full Moon.","A":"Both A and R are individually true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true, but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Oceanography","tags":["Neap Tides","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Neap tides have a smaller tidal range.\nReason — Incorrect: Neap tides occur around the first and third quarter phases, not simply on the New Moon.\nSpring tides occur around the New Moon and Full Moon.","revisionPoints":"Spring tide → maximum tidal range.\nOccurs around:\nNew Moon\nFull Moon\nNeap tide → minimum tidal range.\nOccurs around:\nFirst quarter\nThird quarter\nTides result mainly from the gravitational effects of the Moon and Sun."},
{"no":33,"text":"The prices at which the Government purchases food grains for maintaining the Public Distribution System and building up buffer stocks are known as:","A":"Minimum Support Prices","B":"Procurement Prices","C":"Issue Prices","D":"Ceiling Prices","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Minimum Support Price","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source, the answer is Minimum Support Prices (MSP).\nMSP is announced to provide farmers with a degree of price assurance.\nGovernment procurement of food grains is connected with food-security objectives.","revisionPoints":"MSP → price support mechanism for farmers.\nProcurement → government agencies purchase agricultural produce.\nBuffer stocks → maintained for food security and price stabilisation.\nPublic Distribution System → distribution of essential commodities at controlled/subsidised prices."},
{"no":34,"text":"The theme of the Indian Science Congress, 2001 was:","A":"“Food Nutrition and Environmental Security”","B":"“Arrest declining interest in pure sciences”","C":"“Make India energy self-sufficient”","D":"“Make India IT Superpower”","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science Policy","tags":["Indian Science Congress","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies Food Nutrition and Environmental Security as the theme.\nThe theme reflects the connection between:\nFood availability\nNutrition\nEnvironmental sustainability\nThese issues are closely connected with India's development priorities.","revisionPoints":"Indian Science Congress → major scientific conference in India.\nFood security and environmental security are interconnected.\nAgricultural productivity must be balanced with:\nSoil conservation\nWater management\nBiodiversity\nSustainable resource use"},
{"no":35,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding environmental issues of India:\nGulf of Mannar is one of the biosphere reserves.\nGanga Action Plan Phase II has been merged with the National River Conservation Plan.\nNational Museum of Natural History at New Delhi imparts non-formal education in environment and conservation.\nEnvironmental Information System (ENVIS) acts as a decentralised information network for environmental information.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I, II and IV","B":"I, II, III and IV","C":"II and III","D":"I, III and IV","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Institutions","tags":["ENVIS","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Ganga Action Plan Phase II was integrated into the broader National River Conservation framework.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The National Museum of Natural History promotes environmental education.\nStatement 4 — Correct: ENVIS functions as an environmental information network.","revisionPoints":"Gulf of Mannar → important marine biodiversity region.\nGanga Action Plan → river pollution-control programme.\nENVIS → environmental information network.\nEnvironmental education supports:\nConservation awareness\nSustainable resource use\nBiodiversity protection"},
{"no":36,"text":"Which one of the following Chola kings conquered Ceylon (Sri Lanka)?","A":"Aditya I","B":"Rajaraja I","C":"Rajendra","D":"Vijayalaya","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Chola Empire","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Rajendra Chola I continued the expansionist policy of the Chola Empire.\nHis forces consolidated Chola control over large parts of Sri Lanka.\nThe Cholas developed a powerful military and naval presence.","revisionPoints":"Rajaraja I → major Chola expansion.\nRajendra Chola I → further expansion, including Sri Lanka.\nChola power was centred in Tamil Nadu.\nCholas were notable for:\nTemple architecture\nAdministration\nNaval activity\nOverseas contacts"},
{"no":37,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Armed Forces:\nThe first batch of women pilots was commissioned in the Indian Air Force in 1996.\nOfficers' Training Academy is located in Nagpur.\nSouthern Command of the Indian Navy has its headquarters at Chennai.\nOne of the Regional Headquarters of the Coast Guard is located at Port Blair.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I, II, III and IV","B":"II, III and IV","C":"III only","D":"IV only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Defence Establishments","tags":["Armed Forces","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Officers' Training Academy is not located in Nagpur.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect according to the source.\nStatement 4 — Correct: A Coast Guard regional headquarters is located at Port Blair.\nHence only statement 4 is accepted.","revisionPoints":"Indian Armed Forces include:\nArmy\nNavy\nAir Force\nIndian Coast Guard is responsible for maritime law enforcement and related duties.\nPort Blair is strategically important for India's maritime security because of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.\nDefence-organisation questions often test headquarters and command structures."},
{"no":38,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the power sector in India:\nInstalled capacity of power generation is around 95,000 MW.\nNuclear plants contribute nearly 15% of total power generation.\nHydroelectricity plants contribute nearly 40% of total power generation.\nThermal plants account for nearly 80% of total power generation.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II and III","C":"III and IV","D":"I and IV","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy Policy","tags":["Power Sector","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source, Statements II and III are the correct combination.\nThe question tests the relative contribution of different sources to India's electricity generation.\nIt reflects the energy mix prevailing around the examination period.","revisionPoints":"Major electricity sources include:\nThermal power\nHydropower\nNuclear power\nRenewables\nIndia's energy mix has changed substantially over time.\nHydropower is a renewable source but depends strongly on water availability.\nNuclear power provides base-load electricity with relatively low operational carbon emissions."},
{"no":39,"text":"If a new State of the Indian Union is to be created, which one of the following Schedules of the Constitution must be amended?","A":"First","B":"Second","C":"Third","D":"Fifth","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["First Schedule","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The First Schedule contains the names of the States and Union Territories and specifies their territories.\nCreation or alteration of a State therefore requires corresponding changes to the First Schedule.\nThe relevant constitutional procedure is primarily under Article 3.","revisionPoints":"First Schedule → States and Union Territories.\nArticle 3 → formation of new States and alteration of areas, boundaries or names.\nParliament has the power to reorganise States subject to the constitutional procedure.\nSecond Schedule → salaries, allowances and privileges of specified constitutional authorities."},
{"no":40,"text":"The National Agriculture Insurance Scheme, replacing the Comprehensive Crop Insurance Scheme, was introduced in the year:","A":"1997","B":"1998","C":"1999","D":"2000","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["National Agriculture Insurance Scheme","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source, the National Agriculture Insurance Scheme was introduced in 1999.\nIt was intended to provide insurance protection to farmers against crop losses.\nCrop insurance is an important component of agricultural risk management.","revisionPoints":"National Agriculture Insurance Scheme → introduced in 1999 according to the source.\nObjective → protection against crop failure and related agricultural risks.\nAgricultural insurance helps reduce the financial impact of:\nDrought\nFloods\nNatural calamities\nCrop damage\nCrop insurance is an important part of agricultural risk management."},
{"no":41,"text":"Who presided over the Buddhist Council during the reign of Kanishka in Kashmir?","A":"Parsva","B":"Nagarjuna","C":"Sudraka","D":"Vasumitra","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Fourth Buddhist Council","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Buddhist Council under Kanishka was held in Kashmir.\nVasumitra presided over this council.\nThe council is associated with the development of Mahayana Buddhism.","revisionPoints":"Kanishka: Kushana ruler.\nFourth Buddhist Council: Traditionally associated with Kanishka.\nLocation: Kashmir.\nPresiding figure: Vasumitra.\nMahayana: Received significant royal patronage during the Kushana period."},
{"no":42,"text":"Match the following countries with their Presidents:\nCountry\nPresident\nI. Colombia\nA. Vicente Fox\nII. Philippines\nB. Hugo Chavez\nIII. Mexico\nC. Gloria Macapagal Arroyo\nIV. Venezuela\nD. Andres Pastrana\nCodes:","A":"ID, IIC, IIIB, IVA","B":"IC, IID, IIIB, IVA","C":"ID, IIC, IIIA, IVB","D":"IC, IID, IIIA, IVB","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Latin America","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Colombia → Andres Pastrana\nPhilippines → Gloria Macapagal Arroyo\nMexico → Vicente Fox\nVenezuela → Hugo Chavez","revisionPoints":"Vicente Fox: Mexico.\nHugo Chavez: Venezuela.\nGloria Macapagal Arroyo: Philippines.\nAndres Pastrana: Colombia.\nSuch questions test contemporary international affairs and country–leader associations."},
{"no":43,"text":"The annual agricultural production of a product for the period 1991–92 to 1998–99 is shown in the figure given in the source.\nWhich one of the following is the product represented by Q?","A":"Pulses","B":"Wheat","C":"Oilseeds","D":"Rice","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Rice Production","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source answer identifies Q as Rice.\nThe question depends on interpreting the annual agricultural production graph provided with the original paper.\nThe graph itself is not reproduced in the extracted text.","revisionPoints":"Agricultural production questions often require:\nTrend analysis.\nComparison of annual production.\nRecognition of major crop patterns.\nMajor food crops include rice and wheat.\nPulses and oilseeds have different production patterns from major cereals."},
{"no":44,"text":"Consider the following States:\nI. GujaratII. KarnatakaIII. MaharashtraIV. Tamil Nadu\nThe descending order of these States with reference to their level of Per Capita Net State Domestic Product is:","A":"I, III, IV, II","B":"III, I, II, IV","C":"I, III, II, IV","D":"III, I, IV, II","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"State Economies","tags":["Per Capita NSDP","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Maharashtra → III\nGujarat → I\nKarnataka → II\nTamil Nadu → IV\nTherefore, the descending order is III → I → II → IV.","revisionPoints":"NSDP: Net State Domestic Product.\nPer capita NSDP: NSDP divided by population.\nIt is used as an indicator of the average economic output/income level of a State.\nDo not confuse NSDP with GSDP."},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding political parties in India:\nI. The Representation of the People Act, 1951 provides for the registration of political parties.II. Registration of political parties is carried out by the Election Commission.III. A national-level political party is one which is recognised in four or more States.IV. During the 1999 general elections, there were six National and 48 State-level parties recognised by the Election Commission.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I, II and IV","B":"I and III","C":"II and IV","D":"I, II, III and IV","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Parties","tags":["Election Commission","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement I: Correct according to the source.\nStatement II: Correct — the Election Commission deals with registration and recognition of political parties.\nStatement III: Correct according to the framework used in the source.\nStatement IV: Correct according to the 1999 election context given in the source.","revisionPoints":"Political parties are registered under the Representation of the People Act, 1951.\nElection Commission of India (ECI): Constitutional body responsible for elections.\nDistinguish:\nRegistration of a party\nRecognition as a State/National party\nRecognition criteria have changed over time."},
{"no":46,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nInstitute\nLocation\nI. Central Institute of Medicinal and Aromatic Plants\nA. Chandigarh\nII. Centre for DNA Finger Printing and Diagnostics\nB. Hyderabad\nIII. Institute of Microbial Technology\nC. New Delhi\nIV. National Institute of Immunology\nD. Lucknow\nCodes:","A":"IB, IID, IIIA, IVC","B":"ID, IIB, IIIA, IVC","C":"IB, IID, IIIC, IVA","D":"ID, IIB, IIIC, IVA","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Research Institutions","tags":["Research Institutes","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"CIMAP → Lucknow\nCDFD → Hyderabad\nInstitute of Microbial Technology → Chandigarh\nNational Institute of Immunology → New Delhi\nTherefore, the correct code is ID, IIB, IIIA, IVC.","revisionPoints":"CIMAP: Lucknow.\nCDFD: Hyderabad.\nInstitute of Microbial Technology: Chandigarh.\nNational Institute of Immunology: New Delhi.\nLocation-based questions are common in Science & Technology."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which one of the following animals was NOT represented on the seals and terracotta art of the Harappan culture?","A":"Cow","B":"Elephant","C":"Rhinoceros","D":"Tiger","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Indus Valley Civilization","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Harappan seals and terracotta objects commonly represented animals.\nElephant, rhinoceros and tiger are represented in Harappan artistic remains.\nThe source identifies cow as the animal not represented.","revisionPoints":"Important Harappan animal representations:\nHumped bull\nElephant\nRhinoceros\nTiger\nThe unicorn motif is especially famous in Harappan seals.\nSeals provide important evidence about Harappan culture."},
{"no":48,"text":"The range of Agni-II missile is around:","A":"500 km","B":"2000 km","C":"3500 km","D":"5000 km","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["Agni Missile","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source gives the approximate range of Agni-II as 2,000 km.\nAgni-II is a ballistic missile developed by India.\nThe Agni series is associated with India's strategic missile programme.","revisionPoints":"Agni series: Ballistic missile family.\nAgni-II: Approximately 2,000 km in the context of this 2001 question.\nImportant areas:\nMissile technology\nStrategic defence\nDRDO\nMissile ranges and classifications can change with technological developments."},
{"no":49,"text":"The approximate age of the Aravalli Range is:","A":"370 million years","B":"470 million years","C":"570 million years","D":"670 million years","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Aravalli Range","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source gives 370 million years as the answer.\nThe Aravalli system is one of the very old geological mountain systems.\nIt extends broadly across Rajasthan and adjoining regions.","revisionPoints":"Aravalli Range: Ancient mountain system.\nMajor geographical association: Rajasthan.\nImportant for:\nMineral resources.\nDrainage.\nRegional geography.\nGeological-age questions require careful attention to the source/reference used."},
{"no":50,"text":"Consider the following:\nI. Market borrowingII. Treasury billsIII. Special securities issued to RBI\nWhich of these are components of internal debt?","A":"I only","B":"I and II","C":"II only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Internal Debt","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Market borrowing is a major component of government internal borrowing.\nTreasury Bills are short-term government borrowing instruments.\nSpecial securities issued to RBI are also included in internal debt in the source framework.\nHence, all three are included.","revisionPoints":"Internal debt: Borrowing raised within the country.\nMajor instruments:\nGovernment securities\nTreasury Bills\nOther securities\nExternal debt: Borrowing from foreign sources.\nDistinguish internal debt from total liabilities of government."},
{"no":51,"text":"Match the following Articles of the Constitution with their contents:\nArticle\nContent\nI. Article 54\nA. Election of the President of India\nII. Article 75\nB. Appointment of the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers\nIII. Article 155\nC. Appointment of the Governor of a State\nIV. Article 164\nD. Appointment of the Chief Minister and Council of Ministers of a State\nCodes:","A":"IA, IIB, IIIC, IVD","B":"IA, IIB, IIID, IVE","C":"IB, IIA, IIIC, IVE","D":"IB, IIA, IIID, IVC","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Executive Appointments","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Article 54 → Election of President\nArticle 75 → Prime Minister and Union Council of Ministers\nArticle 155 → Appointment of Governor\nArticle 164 → Chief Minister and State Council of Ministers","revisionPoints":"Article 54: Election of President.\nArticle 75: Union Council of Ministers.\nArticle 155: Governor's appointment.\nArticle 164: State Council of Ministers.\nRemember the distinction between Articles 75 and 164."},
{"no":52,"text":"Match the following important days with their dates:\nImportant Day\nDate\nI. World Environment Day\nA. March 20\nII. World Forestry Day\nB. June 5\nIII. World Habitat Day\nC. September 16\nIV. World Ozone Day\nD. October 3\nCodes:","A":"IB, IIA, IIID, IVE","B":"IA, IIB, IIID, IVC","C":"IA, IIB, IIIC, IVD","D":"IB, IIA, IIID, IVC","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Important Days","tags":["World Environment Day","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"World Environment Day → June 5\nWorld Forestry Day → March 20\nWorld Habitat Day → October 3 according to the source's date framework.\nWorld Ozone Day → September 16","revisionPoints":"June 5: World Environment Day.\nSeptember 16: International Day for the Preservation of the Ozone Layer.\nMarch 20: World Forestry Day in the source.\nImportant-day questions require attention to exact dates."},
{"no":53,"text":"The map in the original paper shows a shaded empire. Which ruler's empire is represented?","A":"Akbar","B":"Aurangzeb","C":"Shah Jahan","D":"Humayun","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Empire","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies the shaded empire as that of Shah Jahan.\nThe question depends on interpreting the map provided in the original paper.\nThe extracted text does not contain the actual map.","revisionPoints":"Shah Jahan: Mughal emperor.\nReigned 1628–1658.\nFamous architectural works include:\nTaj Mahal\nRed Fort\nJama Masjid\nMughal territorial questions often require map-based identification."},
{"no":54,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Reserve Bank of India:\nI. It is a banker to the Central Government.II. It formulates and administers monetary policy.III. It acts as an agent of the Government in respect of India's membership of IMF.IV. It handles the borrowing programme of Government of India.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I and II","B":"II, III and IV","C":"I, II, III and IV","D":"III and IV","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Reserve Bank of India","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement I: Correct — RBI acts as banker to the Central Government.\nStatement II: Correct according to the source.\nThe source's answer is I and II.\nThe question is framed around RBI's monetary and government-banking functions.","revisionPoints":"RBI is India's central bank.\nMajor functions include:\nMonetary policy.\nBanker to government.\nBanker to banks.\nCurrency management.\nRegulation of banking system.\nRBI was established in 1935.\nIt was nationalised in 1949."},
{"no":55,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Most magmas are a combination of liquid, solid and gas.II. Water vapour and carbon dioxide are the principal gases dissolved in magma.III. Basaltic magma is hotter than silicic magma.IV. Magma solidified between sedimentary rocks in a horizontal position is known as a dike.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I, II and III","B":"II, III and IV","C":"I and IV","D":"I, II and IV","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Magma","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement II: Water vapour and carbon dioxide are important gases associated with magma.\nStatement III: Basaltic magma generally has a higher temperature than silicic magma.\nStatement IV: The source identifies the described intrusion as a dike.\nThe source answer is II, III and IV.","revisionPoints":"Magma: Molten material beneath Earth's surface.\nBasaltic magma: Generally hotter and less viscous.\nSilicic magma: Higher silica content and generally more viscous.\nDike: Tabular igneous intrusion cutting across rock layers."},
{"no":56,"text":"Consider the following schemes launched by the Union Government:\nI. Antyodaya AnnaII. Gram Sadak YojanaIII. SarvapriyaIV. Jawahar Gram Samriddhi Yojana\nWhich of these were announced in the year 2000?","A":"I and II","B":"II and IV","C":"III and IV","D":"I, II and III","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Antyodaya Anna Yojana","PMGSY","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies:\nAntyodaya Anna Yojana → 2000\nPradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana → 2000\nTherefore, the correct combination is I and II.","revisionPoints":"Antyodaya Anna Yojana: Focused on food security for the poorest households.\nGram Sadak Yojana: Rural road connectivity.\nScheme-based questions require attention to:\nLaunch year.\nTarget beneficiaries.\nMinistry/department.\nMain objective."},
{"no":57,"text":"Match the following books with their authors:\nBook\nAuthor\nI. The Struggle in My Life\nA. Lech Walesa\nII. The Struggle and the Triumph\nB. Nelson Mandela\nIII. Friends and Foes\nC. Leonid Brezhnev\nIV. Rebirth\nD. Zulfikar Ali Bhutto\nE. Sheikh Mujibur Rehman\nCodes:","A":"IA, IIB, IIIE, IVC","B":"IB, IIA, IIIE, IVC","C":"IA, IIB, IIIC, IVD","D":"IB, IIA, IIIC, IVD","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Books & Authors","tags":["World Leader Memoirs","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Struggle in My Life → Nelson Mandela\nThe Struggle and the Triumph → Lech Walesa\nFriends and Foes → Sheikh Mujibur Rehman\nRebirth → Leonid Brezhnev","revisionPoints":"Nelson Mandela: South African anti-apartheid leader.\nLech Walesa: Polish political leader and Solidarity movement figure.\nSheikh Mujibur Rehman: Major leader in the history of Bangladesh.\nBook–author questions often connect contemporary history with personalities."},
{"no":58,"text":"Hoysala monuments are found in:","A":"Hampi and Hospet","B":"Halebid and Belur","C":"Mysore and Bangalore","D":"Sringeri and Dharwar","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Hoysala Architecture","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Halebid and Belur are major centres of Hoysala architecture.\nHoysala temples are noted for:\nDetailed sculptures.\nIntricate carvings.\nStar-shaped architectural plans.\nThey are located in present-day Karnataka.","revisionPoints":"Hoysala architecture: Karnataka.\nImportant sites:\nBelur\nHalebid\nFamous temples:\nChennakeshava Temple, Belur.\nHoysaleswara Temple, Halebid.\nDistinguish Hoysala architecture from Vijayanagara architecture at Hampi."},
{"no":59,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding earthquakes:\nI. The intensity of an earthquake is measured on the Mercalli scale.II. The magnitude of an earthquake is a measure of energy released.III. Earthquake magnitudes are based on direct measurements of the amplitude of seismic waves.IV. In the Richter scale, each whole number demonstrates a hundredfold increase in the amount of energy released.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I, II and III","B":"II, III and IV","C":"I and IV","D":"I and III","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Richter Scale","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement II: Magnitude relates to the energy released by an earthquake.\nStatement III: Magnitude calculations use measurements of seismic waves.\nThe source marks II, III and IV as correct.\nSource note: The statement about a hundredfold increase in energy for each Richter unit is technically problematic; the source key should therefore be treated cautiously.","revisionPoints":"Magnitude: Quantifies earthquake size/energy.\nIntensity: Describes observed effects/damage at a location.\nMercalli scale: Measures intensity.\nRichter scale: Historically used to express earthquake magnitude.\nDo not confuse magnitude with intensity."},
{"no":60,"text":"Which Article of the Constitution provides that it shall be the endeavour of every State to provide adequate facilities for instruction in the mother tongue at the primary stage of education?","A":"Article 349","B":"Article 350","C":"Article 350A","D":"Article 351","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Linguistic Provisions","tags":["Article 350-A","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Article 350A deals with facilities for instruction in the mother tongue at the primary stage for children belonging to linguistic minority groups.\nIt is associated with the constitutional protection of linguistic minorities.","revisionPoints":"Article 350: Right to submit representations for redress of grievances in any language used by the Union or State.\nArticle 350A: Mother-tongue instruction at primary stage for linguistic minorities.\nArticle 350B: Special Officer for linguistic minorities.\nArticle 351: Directive for development of Hindi."},
{"no":61,"text":"Who among the following Indian rulers established embassies in foreign countries on modern lines?","A":"Haider Ali","B":"Mir Qasim","C":"Shah Alam II","D":"Tipu Sultan","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Tipu Sultan","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Tipu Sultan established diplomatic relations with foreign powers.\nHe sent missions/embassies to countries outside India.\nHis foreign policy was closely connected with opposition to the British.","revisionPoints":"Tipu Sultan: Ruler of Mysore.\nSon of Haider Ali.\nFought the British in the Anglo-Mysore Wars.\nKnown for diplomatic contacts with foreign powers.\nDied in 1799 at Srirangapatna."},
{"no":62,"text":"A great landslide caused by an earthquake killed hundreds of people in January 2001 near:","A":"San Salvador","B":"San Jose","C":"Managua","D":"Guatemala City","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["El Salvador","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies San Salvador as the location.\nThe event was associated with a major earthquake and resulting landslides.\nIt illustrates the secondary hazards that can follow earthquakes.","revisionPoints":"Earthquakes can cause:\nLandslides\nTsunamis\nBuilding collapse\nSoil liquefaction\nSan Salvador: Capital of El Salvador.\nCentral America is part of a seismically active region."},
{"no":63,"text":"In the map given in the original paper, the black marks show the distribution of:","A":"Asbestos","B":"Gypsum","C":"Limestone","D":"Mica","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Minerals","tags":["Limestone","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies the black-marked areas as the distribution of limestone.\nThis question depends on interpreting the map provided in the original paper.\nThe actual map is not present in the extracted text.","revisionPoints":"Limestone: Important non-metallic mineral.\nMajor uses:\nCement production\nConstruction\nChemical industries\nIron and steel industry as a flux\nMineral-distribution questions are often map-based."},
{"no":64,"text":"Consider the following factors regarding an industry:\nI. Capital investmentII. Business turnoverIII. Labour forceIV. Power consumption\nWhich of these determine the nature and size of the industry?","A":"I, III and IV","B":"I, II and IV","C":"II, III and IV","D":"II and III","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industry","tags":["Industrial Classification","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies capital investment, business turnover and power consumption as the relevant factors.\nThese indicators help classify and understand the scale and characteristics of industrial activity.","revisionPoints":"Industrial classification can involve:\nCapital investment.\nProduction/turnover.\nEnergy consumption.\nEmployment.\nSmall-scale vs large-scale industry can be distinguished using multiple indicators.\nIndustrial geography also considers raw materials, labour, power and markets."},
{"no":65,"text":"Which of the following pairs is NOT correctly matched?","A":"India's first Technicolor film — Jhansi Ki Rani","B":"India's first 3D film — My Dear Kuttichathan","C":"India's first insured film — Taal","D":"India's first actress to win the Bharat Ratna — Meena Kumari","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Cinema","tags":["Indian Cinema","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies (d) as the incorrect pair.\nMeena Kumari did not receive the Bharat Ratna.\nTherefore, the pair is not correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Meena Kumari: Famous Hindi film actress.\nMy Dear Kuttichathan: Associated with India's early 3D cinema.\nQuestions may test:\nFirsts in Indian cinema.\nAwards.\nPersonalities and achievements."},
{"no":66,"text":"Match the following dancers with their dance forms:\nDancer\nDance\nI. Kalamandalam Kshemavaty\nA. Kathakali\nII. Kottakkal Sivaraman\nB. Manipuri\nIII. Lakshmi Viswanathan\nC. Mohiniyattam\nIV. N. Madhabi Devi\nD. Bharata Natyam\nCodes:","A":"IA, IIC, IIIB, IVD","B":"IC, IIA, IIID, IVB","C":"IA, IIC, IIID, IVB","D":"IC, IIA, IIIB, IVD","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Performing Arts","tags":["Classical Dance","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Kalamandalam Kshemavaty → Mohiniyattam\nKottakkal Sivaraman → Kathakali\nLakshmi Viswanathan → Bharata Natyam\nN. Madhabi Devi → Manipuri","revisionPoints":"Mohiniyattam: Kerala.\nKathakali: Kerala.\nBharata Natyam: Tamil Nadu.\nManipuri: Manipur.\nLearn classical dances along with their States of origin and major exponents."},
{"no":67,"text":"Match the Bhakti saints with their professions:\nBhakti Saint\nProfession\nI. Namdev\nA. Barber\nII. Kabir\nB. Weaver\nIII. Ravidas\nC. Tailor\nIV. Sena\nD. Cobbler\nCodes:","A":"IB, IIC, IIIA, IVD","B":"IC, IIB, IIID, IVA","C":"IC, IIB, IIIA, IVD","D":"IB, IIC, IIID, IVA","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Bhakti Movement","tags":["Bhakti Saints","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Namdev → Tailor\nKabir → Weaver\nRavidas → Cobbler\nSena → Barber\nThe question highlights the social background of Bhakti saints.","revisionPoints":"Kabir: Weaver; opposed religious orthodoxy and emphasised devotion.\nRavidas: Associated with the leather-working/cobbler community.\nSena: Associated with barber profession.\nBhakti movement challenged aspects of caste and ritualism."},
{"no":68,"text":"The most appropriate measure of a country's economic growth is its:","A":"Gross Domestic Product","B":"Net Domestic Product","C":"Net National Product","D":"Per Capita Real Income","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"National Income","tags":["Per Capita Income","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies Per Capita Real Income.\n“Real” adjusts for changes in prices.\n“Per capita” takes population into account.\nTherefore, it provides a better indication of changes in average real economic well-being.","revisionPoints":"GDP: Value of final goods and services produced within a country.\nPer capita income: Income/output divided by population.\nReal income: Adjusted for price changes.\nEconomic growth should be distinguished from economic development."},
{"no":69,"text":"The Supreme Court of India tenders advice to the President on a matter of law or fact:","A":"On its own initiative","B":"Only if he seeks such advice","C":"Only if the matter relates to Fundamental Rights of citizens","D":"Only if the issue poses a threat to the unity and integrity of the country","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Presidential Reference","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Under the advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court, the President may refer a question of law or fact to the Court.\nThe Court then gives its opinion.\nThe source identifies (b) as correct.","revisionPoints":"Article 143: Advisory jurisdiction of Supreme Court.\nPresident may refer questions of:\nLaw\nFact\nPublic importance\nThis is different from the Supreme Court's original and appellate jurisdiction."},
{"no":70,"text":"Falun Gong is:","A":"An ethnic minority in Eastern China","B":"An insurgency outfit in Western China","C":"A pro-democracy movement in China","D":"A spiritual movement in China","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Falun Gong","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source describes Falun Gong as a spiritual movement.\nIt originated in China and became prominent during the 1990s.\nThe Chinese government subsequently treated the movement as a major political/social challenge.","revisionPoints":"Falun Gong: Spiritual movement originating in China.\nAssociated with meditation and spiritual practices.\nBecame an important contemporary-affairs issue in China in the late 1990s.\nContemporary questions may link social movements with geopolitical developments."},
{"no":71,"text":"The Hunter Commission was appointed after the:","A":"Black Hole incident","B":"Jallianwala Bagh massacre","C":"Uprising of 1857","D":"Partition of Bengal","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Hunter Commission","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Jallianwala Bagh massacre occurred at Amritsar in 1919.\nGeneral Reginald Dyer ordered troops to fire on the gathering.\nThe British Government appointed the Hunter Committee/Commission to investigate the incident.","revisionPoints":"Year: 1919.\nPlace: Jallianwala Bagh, Amritsar.\nGeneral Dyer: Ordered firing.\nHunter Commission: Investigated the events.\nThe massacre intensified anti-British sentiment."},
{"no":72,"text":"Who among the following was the first to state that the Earth was spherical?","A":"Aristotle","B":"Copernicus","C":"Ptolemy","D":"Strabo","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"World History","tags":["History of Science","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source gives Ptolemy as the answer.\nPtolemy was a prominent Greco-Roman astronomer and geographer.\nHis works greatly influenced geographical thought for centuries.","revisionPoints":"Ptolemy: Famous astronomer and geographer.\nAssociated with the geocentric model.\nCopernicus: Later proposed the heliocentric model.\nAristotle: Also provided arguments supporting a spherical Earth.\nSource note: The historical wording of this question is debatable; the answer above follows the supplied source."},
{"no":73,"text":"Match the following terms with their explanations:\nTerm","A":"Option A","B":"Option B","C":"Option C","D":"Option D","ans":null,"category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Fiscal Deficit","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"I. Fiscal deficit\nA. Excess of Total Expenditure over Total Receipts\nII. Budget deficit\nB. Excess of Revenue Expenditure over Revenue Receipts\nIII. Revenue deficit\nC. Excess of Total Expenditure over Total Receipts less borrowings\nIV. Primary deficit\nD. Excess of Total Expenditure over Total Receipts less borrowings and Interest Payments\nCodes:\n(a) IC, IIA, IIIB, IVD(b) ID, IIC, IIIB, IVA(c) IA, IIC, IIIB, IVD(d) IC, IIA, IIID, IVB\nFiscal deficit → C\nBudget deficit → A\nRevenue deficit → B\nPrimary deficit → D\nPrimary deficit is essentially the fiscal deficit excluding interest payments.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal deficit: Total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings.\nRevenue deficit: Revenue expenditure minus revenue receipts.\nPrimary deficit: Fiscal deficit minus interest payments.\nBudget deficit: Broader/older concept comparing total expenditure and total receipts.","dropped":true},
{"no":74,"text":"Consider the following organisations:\nI. International Bank for Reconstruction and DevelopmentII. International Finance CorporationIII. International Fund for Agricultural DevelopmentIV. International Monetary Fund\nWhich of these are agencies of the United Nations?","A":"I and II","B":"II and III","C":"III and IV","D":"I, II, III and IV","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"United Nations","tags":["UN Specialized Agencies","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The supplied source marks II and III as the answer.\nThe question concerns the institutional relationship between international financial organisations and the UN system.\nThis is a source-key-based answer.","revisionPoints":"IBRD: Part of the World Bank Group.\nIFC: Part of the World Bank Group.\nIFAD: International organisation focused on rural development and agricultural development.\nIMF: International monetary and financial institution.\nSource note: The institutional classification in this question is dated and can be framed differently under modern UN-system terminology."},
{"no":75,"text":"Match the following persons with their achievements:\nPerson\nAchievement\nI. Deep Sen Gupta\nA. India's youngest International Master in Sangli International Chess Tournament, 2000\nII. P. Hari Krishna\nB. First Indian to win gold in discus in the World Athletic Championship in Santiago, 2000\nIII. Seema Antil\nC. Won the Asian Junior Chess Tournament in Mumbai, 2000\nIV. Tejas Bakre\nD. Won the Under-12 title in the World Youth Chess Festival in Oropesa, 2000\nCodes:","A":"ID, IIC, IIIB, IVA","B":"IB, IID, IIIA, IVC","C":"ID, IIA, IIIB, IVC","D":"IB, IIA, IIIC, IVD","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Chess","Athletics","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Deep Sen Gupta → Under-12 World Youth Chess Festival\nP. Hari Krishna → Asian Junior Chess Tournament\nSeema Antil → Discus gold\nTejas Bakre → Youngest International Master achievement","revisionPoints":"Questions on sports personalities often test:\nAchievement.\nEvent.\nYear.\nLocation.\nSeema Antil: Associated with discus throw.\nChess-related questions may involve titles such as International Master."},
{"no":76,"text":"Under the Permanent Settlement of 1793, zamindars were required to issue pattas to farmers, but many zamindars did not issue them. The reason was:","A":"The zamindars were trusted by the farmers","B":"There was no official check upon the zamindars","C":"It was the responsibility of the British Government","D":"The farmers were not interested in getting pattas","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Permanent Settlement","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Permanent Settlement recognised zamindars as revenue intermediaries/landholders.\nPattas were intended to specify tenants' rights and obligations.\nThe source states that lack of effective official checking enabled many zamindars to avoid issuing them.","revisionPoints":"Permanent Settlement: Introduced in 1793 under Lord Cornwallis.\nMainly associated with Bengal, Bihar and parts of Odisha.\nZamindars were responsible for paying fixed land revenue to the government.\nImportant concepts:\nZamindari\nRyot\nPatta\nRevenue settlement"},
{"no":77,"text":"If the stars are seen to rise perpendicular to the horizon by an observer, he is located on the:","A":"Equator","B":"Tropic of Cancer","C":"South Pole","D":"North Pole","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Celestial Observation","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"At the Equator, celestial bodies appear to rise approximately perpendicular to the horizon.\nThis is related to the orientation of Earth's rotational axis relative to the horizon.\nAt the poles, celestial bodies have very different apparent paths.","revisionPoints":"Equator: 0° latitude.\nEarth rotates from west to east.\nStars appear to move across the sky because of Earth's rotation.\nApparent stellar motion varies with latitude."},
{"no":78,"text":"Match the following persons with what they are distinguished for:\nPerson\nDistinguished as\nI. Santosh Yadav\nA. TV Host\nII. Oprah Winfrey\nB. Journalist\nIII. Oscar Wilde\nC. Mountaineer\nIV. P. Sainath\nD. Dramatist and Author\nCodes:","A":"IC, IIA, IIID, IVB","B":"IA, IIC, IIIB, IVD","C":"IC, IIA, IIIB, IVD","D":"IA, IIC, IIID, IVB","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Personalities","tags":["Notable Personalities","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Santosh Yadav → Mountaineer\nOprah Winfrey → TV Host\nOscar Wilde → Dramatist and Author\nP. Sainath → Journalist","revisionPoints":"Santosh Yadav: Indian mountaineer.\nOprah Winfrey: Television personality and host.\nOscar Wilde: Irish writer and dramatist.\nP. Sainath: Indian journalist.\nPersonality questions often combine India and international figures."},
{"no":79,"text":"The shaded map in the original paper represents an area with a mean July temperature of 25°C–27.5°C.\nWhich option corresponds to the shaded region?","A":"Region I","B":"Region II","C":"Region III","D":"Region IV","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Temperature Isotherms","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies Region II as the correct map area.\nThe question requires interpretation of the original temperature-distribution map.\nThe extracted text does not contain the visual map itself.","revisionPoints":"July temperature: Important for understanding India's summer climatic conditions.\nTemperature distribution depends on:\nLatitude\nAltitude\nDistance from sea\nRelief\nMonsoon conditions\nMap-based climatology questions require careful spatial interpretation."},
{"no":80,"text":"The Union Budget of 2000 provided a tax holiday in the North-Eastern region for:","A":"5 years","B":"7 years","C":"8 years","D":"10 years","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industrial Policy","tags":["North East Tax Holiday","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source states that the tax holiday was provided for 10 years.\nSuch incentives were intended to encourage industrial development and investment in the North-Eastern region.","revisionPoints":"Regional tax incentives are used to promote:\nIndustrialisation\nEmployment\nInvestment\nBalanced regional development\nNorth-Eastern India has historically received special development policies.\nIn budget questions, remember the objective as well as the duration of an incentive."},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Arya Samaj was founded in 1835.II. Lala Lajpat Rai opposed the appeal of Arya Samaj to the authority of Vedas in support of its social reform programmes.III. Under Keshab Chandra Sen, the Brahmo Samaj campaigned for women’s education.IV. Vinoba Bhave founded the Sarvodaya Samaj to work among refugees.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I and II","B":"II and III","C":"II and IV","D":"III and IV","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Social Reform Movement","tags":["Arya Samaj","Brahmo Samaj","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement I — Incorrect: The source states that the founding year given is incorrect.\nStatement II — Correct according to the source.\nStatement III — Correct: Keshab Chandra Sen was associated with social reform activities including women's education.\nStatement IV — Incorrect according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Arya Samaj: Founded by Swami Dayanand Saraswati.\nBrahmo Samaj: Important reform movement of 19th-century India.\nKeshab Chandra Sen: Important Brahmo reformer.\nLala Lajpat Rai: Associated with the Arya Samaj and nationalist movement.\nVinoba Bhave: Major figure of the Bhoodan/Sarvodaya movement."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following taxes:\nI. Corporation taxII. Customs dutyIII. Wealth taxIV. Excise duty\nWhich of these are indirect taxes?","A":"I only","B":"II and IV","C":"I and III","D":"II and III","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Indirect Taxes","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Customs duty → Indirect tax\nExcise duty → Indirect tax\nCorporation tax and wealth tax are treated as direct taxes.\nTherefore, the correct combination is II and IV.","revisionPoints":"Direct tax: Burden generally falls directly on the taxpayer.\nIndirect tax: Burden can be shifted to another person, usually through prices.\nExamples:\nDirect: Income tax, corporation tax.\nIndirect: Customs duty, excise duty.\nGST is a major modern example of an indirect tax."},
{"no":83,"text":"The Mongols under Genghis Khan invaded India during the reign of:","A":"Balban","B":"Feroze Tughlaq","C":"Iltutmish","D":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mongol Invasions","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies Iltutmish as the ruler during whose reign the Mongol threat reached India.\nThe Mongol expansion under Genghis Khan created a major geopolitical challenge for Central and South Asia.\nIltutmish adopted a cautious policy toward the Mongol situation.","revisionPoints":"Iltutmish: Important ruler of the Delhi Sultanate.\nDynasty: Slave/Mamluk Dynasty.\nMongols originated in Central Asia.\nGenghis Khan: Founder of the Mongol Empire.\nMongol invasions became a recurring challenge for later Delhi Sultans."},
{"no":84,"text":"The high density of population in the Nile Valley and the island of Java is primarily due to:","A":"Intensive agriculture","B":"Industrialisation","C":"Urbanisation","D":"Topographic constraints","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Population Geography","tags":["Population Density","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Both regions support large populations through highly productive agriculture.\nThe Nile Valley benefits from water availability and fertile alluvial soils.\nJava has fertile volcanic soils and intensive cultivation.","revisionPoints":"Nile Valley: Population concentrated along the river because of fertile land and water.\nJava: Dense population associated with fertile volcanic soils and intensive agriculture.\nPopulation density is influenced by:\nWater availability.\nFertility of soil.\nClimate.\nEconomic opportunities."},
{"no":85,"text":"The largest share of Foreign Direct Investment (1997–2000) went to:","A":"Food and food product sector","B":"Engineering sector","C":"Electronics and electrical equipment sector","D":"Service sector","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Foreign Direct Investment","tags":["FDI","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"According to the source, the engineering sector received the largest share.\nFDI represents investment by foreign entities in productive activities in another country.\nSectoral distribution can indicate areas attracting international investment.","revisionPoints":"FDI: Foreign investment involving a lasting interest/control in an enterprise.\nFDI can bring:\nCapital\nTechnology\nManagement expertise\nAccess to international markets\nDistinguish FDI from Foreign Portfolio Investment (FPI)."},
{"no":86,"text":"Which one of the following duties is NOT performed by the Comptroller and Auditor General of India (CAG)?","A":"To audit and report on all expenditure from the Consolidated Fund of India","B":"To audit and report on all expenditure from the Contingency Funds and Public Accounts","C":"To audit and report on all trading, manufacturing, profit and loss accounts","D":"To control the receipt and issue of public money, and ensure that public revenue is lodged in the exchequer","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"CAG","tags":["Comptroller and Auditor General","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The CAG is an auditing authority, not the authority that directly controls the receipt and issue of public money.\nIt examines government accounts and expenditure.\nTherefore, (d) is not a CAG function.","revisionPoints":"CAG: Constitutional authority under Article 148.\nAudits:\nGovernment expenditure.\nGovernment accounts.\nReceipts and expenditure.\nCAG reports are submitted to the President/Governor, depending on the account concerned.\nCAG is important for legislative financial control."},
{"no":87,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Harappan Civilisation — Painted Grey Ware","B":"The Kushans — Gandhara School of Art","C":"The Mughals — Ajanta Paintings","D":"The Marathas — Pahari School of Painting","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient & Medieval India","tags":["Gandhara Art","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Gandhara School of Art flourished particularly under Kushana patronage.\nIt is known for strong Greco-Roman artistic influence.\nThe other pairs do not correctly associate the traditions with these political/cultural periods.","revisionPoints":"Gandhara School: Strong Greco-Roman influence.\nMathura School: Indigenous Indian artistic tradition.\nKushanas: Important patrons of Buddhist art.\nPainted Grey Ware: Associated with a much later archaeological phase than the Harappan civilisation."},
{"no":88,"text":"Consider the following statements about sedimentary rocks:\nI. Sedimentary rocks are formed at Earth's surface by the hydrological system.II. Their formation involves weathering of pre-existing rocks.III. Sedimentary rocks contain fossils.IV. Sedimentary rocks typically occur in layers.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"I and II","B":"I and IV","C":"II, III and IV","D":"I, II, III and IV","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Sedimentary Rocks","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Sedimentary rocks are formed through deposition and lithification of sediments.\nWeathering of pre-existing rocks contributes sediment.\nSedimentary layers can preserve fossils.\nThey commonly occur in strata/layers.","revisionPoints":"Three major rock types:\nIgneous\nSedimentary\nMetamorphic\nSedimentary processes include:\nWeathering\nErosion\nTransportation\nDeposition\nCompaction/cementation\nFossils are commonly preserved in sedimentary rocks."},
{"no":89,"text":"Match the following publishers with their publications:\nPublisher\nPublication\nI. Ministry of Industry\nA. Report on Currency and Finance\nII. Central Statistical Organisation\nB. Economic Survey\nIII. Reserve Bank of India\nC. Wholesale Price Index\nIV. Ministry of Finance\nD. National Accounts Statistics\nCodes:","A":"ID, IIC, IIIB, IVA","B":"IC, IID, IIIA, IVB","C":"ID, IIC, IIIA, IVB","D":"IC, IID, IIIB, IVA","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Publications","tags":["Economic Survey","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Ministry of Industry → Wholesale Price Index\nCSO → National Accounts Statistics\nRBI → Report on Currency and Finance\nMinistry of Finance → Economic Survey","revisionPoints":"RBI: Publishes the Report on Currency and Finance.\nCSO: Historically associated with national statistical publications.\nEconomic Survey: Associated with the Ministry of Finance.\nWPI: Government statistical indicator of wholesale price movements."},
{"no":90,"text":"Match the following awards with their recipients:\nAward/Prize\nRecipient\nI. Indira Gandhi Prize for Peace, Disarmament\nA. Archbishop Desmond Tutu\nII. Jamnalal Bajaj Award\nB. Dr. Gurudev Khush\nIII. International Gandhi Peace Prize\nC. Dr. M.S. Swaminathan\nIV. Wolf Prize\nD. Nelson Mandela\nCodes:","A":"IA, IIC, IIID, IVB","B":"IC, IIA, IIID, IVB","C":"IA, IIC, IIIB, IVD","D":"IC, IIA, IIIB, IVD","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Notable Awards","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Indira Gandhi Prize → Dr. M.S. Swaminathan\nJamnalal Bajaj Award → Archbishop Desmond Tutu\nInternational Gandhi Peace Prize → Nelson Mandela\nWolf Prize → Dr. Gurdev/Gurudev Khush","revisionPoints":"M.S. Swaminathan: Agricultural scientist associated with India's Green Revolution.\nNelson Mandela: Anti-apartheid leader and former President of South Africa.\nDesmond Tutu: South African religious and human-rights figure.\nGurdev Khush: Renowned agricultural scientist associated with rice research."},
{"no":91,"text":"Which one of the following statements correctly describes the Fourth Schedule of the Constitution of India?","A":"It lists the distribution of power between the Union and the States","B":"It contains the languages listed in the Constitution","C":"It contains provisions regarding the administration of tribal areas","D":"It allocates seats in the Council of States","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fourth Schedule","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Fourth Schedule deals with the allocation of seats in the Rajya Sabha/Council of States.\nIt specifies the representation of States and certain Union Territories.","revisionPoints":"Fourth Schedule: Allocation of Rajya Sabha seats.\nSeventh Schedule: Union, State and Concurrent Lists.\nEighth Schedule: Recognised languages.\nFifth/Sixth Schedules: Administration of specified tribal areas."},
{"no":92,"text":"Which among the following ports was called Babul Makka (Gate of Makka) during the Mughal period?","A":"Calicut","B":"Broach","C":"Cambay","D":"Surat","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Trade","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Surat was a major Mughal-period port on the western coast.\nIt was an important centre for trade with the Red Sea, Persian Gulf and other regions.\nIts association with pilgrimage routes to Mecca gave rise to the expression Babul Makka.","revisionPoints":"Surat: Major port during the Mughal period.\nImportant centre of:\nOverseas trade.\nTextile commerce.\nPilgrimage traffic.\nWestern Indian ports played an important role in Indian Ocean trade."},
{"no":93,"text":"Volcanic eruptions do not occur in the:","A":"Baltic Sea","B":"Black Sea","C":"Caribbean Sea","D":"Caspian Sea","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Volcanic Activity","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies the Baltic Sea.\nVolcanic activity is associated with tectonically active regions.\nThe Baltic region is not a major zone of active volcanism.","revisionPoints":"Volcanism is strongly associated with:\nPlate boundaries.\nHotspots.\nRift zones.\nMajor volcanic regions include parts of the Pacific Ring of Fire.\nThe Baltic Sea is in northern Europe."},
{"no":94,"text":"Who among the following leaders proposed adopting Complete Independence as the goal of the Congress at the Ahmedabad session of 1920?","A":"Abul Kalam Azad","B":"Hasrat Mohani","C":"Jawaharlal Nehru","D":"Mahatma Gandhi","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Complete Independence","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Hasrat Mohani advocated the goal of complete independence.\nHe was an important early proponent of Purna Swaraj.\nThe demand represented a more radical position than the earlier demand for constitutional reforms or dominion status.","revisionPoints":"Hasrat Mohani: Revolutionary nationalist and poet.\nPurna Swaraj: Complete independence.\nThe Congress formally adopted Purna Swaraj as its goal at the Lahore Session in 1929.\n26 January 1930: Observed as Independence Day by nationalists."},
{"no":95,"text":"Match the following sportswomen with their sports:\nSportswoman\nSport\nI. Anjali Vedpathak\nA. Athletics\nII. Mouma Das\nB. Gymnastics\nIII. Neelam Singh\nC. Rifle Shooting\nIV. Tumpa Debnath\nD. Table Tennis\nE. Chess\nCodes:","A":"IC, IID, IIIB, IVE","B":"ID, IIC, IIIA, IVB","C":"ID, IIC, IIIB, IVE","D":"IC, IID, IIIA, IVB","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Sportswomen","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Anjali Vedpathak → Rifle Shooting\nMouma Das → Table Tennis\nNeelam Singh → Athletics\nTumpa Debnath → Gymnastics","revisionPoints":"Anjali Vedpathak: Shooting.\nMouma Das: Table tennis.\nNeelam Singh: Athletics.\nTumpa Debnath: Gymnastics.\nSports questions may test person–sport–achievement combinations."},
{"no":96,"text":"A class of animals known as Marsupials is a characteristic feature of:","A":"Africa","B":"Australia","C":"South America","D":"South-east Asia","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Marsupials","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Marsupials are mammals whose young undergo an early stage of development outside the uterus, commonly associated with a pouch.\nAustralia has a particularly rich diversity of marsupials.\nExamples include kangaroos, wallabies and koalas.","revisionPoints":"Marsupials: Characteristic of Australian fauna.\nExamples:\nKangaroo\nKoala\nWombat\nWallaby\nAustralia is known for its distinctive endemic fauna."},
{"no":97,"text":"The locations of space-organisation units are marked as 1, 2, 3 and 4 in the map. Match them with:\nA. ISROB. IIRSC. NRSAD. SAC\nThe source gives the following location options:","A":"Calicut","B":"Broach","C":"Cambay","D":"Surat","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Indian Institute of Remote Sensing","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"This is a map-based question from the original paper.\nThe extracted source text does not preserve the actual map, so the exact spatial matching cannot be independently reconstructed from the text alone.\nThe supplied source marks (d) Surat as the answer.","revisionPoints":"ISRO: Indian Space Research Organisation.\nIIRS: Indian Institute of Remote Sensing.\nNRSA: National Remote Sensing Agency — historical institutional name.\nSAC: Space Applications Centre.\nMap questions require identification of institution + location."},
{"no":98,"text":"Which of the following pairs is correctly matched?","A":"Diwan-i-Bandagani — Tughlaq","B":"Diwan-i-Mustakhraj — Balban","C":"Diwan-i-Kohi — Alauddin Khilji","D":"Diwan-i-Arz — Muhammad Tughlaq","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Delhi Sultanate Administration","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Diwan-i-Bandagani was associated with Firoz Shah Tughlaq and his department dealing with slaves.\nThe other departments are incorrectly matched with the rulers in the options.","revisionPoints":"Firoz Shah Tughlaq: Known for establishing a separate department for slaves.\nDiwan-i-Arz: Military department.\nDelhi Sultanate questions often ask about:\nAdministrative departments.\nRevenue reforms.\nMilitary organisation.\nMarket regulations."},
{"no":99,"text":"The earlier name of the WTO was:","A":"UNCTAD","B":"GATT","C":"UNIDO","D":"OECD","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Organizations","tags":["GATT","WTO","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"GATT stands for General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade.\nIt provided the multilateral framework for international trade before the creation of the World Trade Organization (WTO).\nWTO came into existence in 1995.","revisionPoints":"GATT: General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade.\nWTO: Established in 1995.\nWTO headquarters: Geneva.\nMajor WTO areas:\nTrade in goods.\nTrade in services.\nIntellectual property.\nUNCTAD is a separate UN-related organisation dealing with trade and development."},
{"no":100,"text":"Two wires have their lengths, diameters and resistivities all in the ratio 1 : 2. If the resistance of the thinner wire is 10 ohms, the resistance of the thicker wire is:","A":"10 ohms","B":"5 ohms","C":"20 ohms","D":"40 ohms","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Resistance is given by:\nR = ρL/A\nDiameter ratio = 1 : 2.\nTherefore, area ratio = diameter² = 1 : 4.\nFor the thicker wire:\nLength doubles.\nResistivity doubles.\nArea becomes four times.\nTherefore:\nR₂/R₁ = (2 × 2) / 4 = 1\nHowever, following the source's stated answer, the answer is 5 ohms. The source's wording about all three ratios creates a consistency issue with the direct resistance formula.","revisionPoints":"Resistance: \\(R = \\rho L/A\\)\nResistance increases with:\nLength.\nResistivity.\nResistance decreases with:\nCross-sectional area.\nIf diameter doubles, area becomes four times.\nAlways convert diameter ratios into area ratios by squaring."},
{"no":101,"text":"A stick appears bent when partially immersed in water because of:","A":"Reflection","B":"Diffraction","C":"Refraction","D":"Dispersion","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Refraction","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Light travels from water to air when reaching the observer.\nIts speed changes when it passes between different media.\nThis causes the direction of light to change.\nThe apparent position of the submerged part changes.\nTherefore, the stick appears bent.","revisionPoints":"Refraction = bending of light when it passes from one medium to another.\nRefraction occurs because the speed of light changes.\nReflection involves bouncing back of light.\nDispersion separates white light into different colours."},
{"no":102,"text":"A copper vessel feels hotter than a glass vessel when both are kept at the same temperature because:","A":"Copper has a higher temperature than glass","B":"Copper has greater density than glass","C":"Copper conducts heat better than glass","D":"Glass produces cold","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Thermal Conductivity","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Both vessels are assumed to be at the same temperature.\nCopper is a much better conductor of heat than glass.\nWhen touched, copper transfers heat to the hand more rapidly.\nTherefore, copper feels hotter even though both have the same temperature.","revisionPoints":"Thermal conductivity determines how readily heat is transferred.\nMetals generally have high thermal conductivity.\nGlass is a relatively poor conductor of heat.\nFeeling hotter does not necessarily mean that an object has a higher temperature."},
{"no":103,"text":"The boiling point of water decreases with increase in altitude because:","A":"Atmospheric pressure decreases with altitude","B":"Atmospheric pressure increases with altitude","C":"Temperature increases with altitude","D":"Density of water increases with altitude","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Boiling Point","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Boiling occurs when the vapour pressure of a liquid becomes equal to the surrounding pressure.\nAtmospheric pressure decreases as altitude increases.\nTherefore, water reaches its boiling condition at a lower temperature.\nHence, the boiling point decreases with altitude.","revisionPoints":"Higher altitude → lower atmospheric pressure.\nLower pressure → lower boiling point.\nPressure cookers increase pressure and therefore raise the boiling point of water.\nThis principle is important in cooking at high altitudes."},
{"no":104,"text":"When light passes from air into glass, which of the following properties are affected?","A":"Frequency only","B":"Frequency and wavelength","C":"Frequency and velocity","D":"Wavelength and velocity","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Refraction of Light","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"When light enters glass from air, its speed decreases.\nFrequency remains unchanged when light passes from one medium to another.\nSince \\(v = f\\lambda\\), with frequency constant and velocity changing, wavelength also changes.\nTherefore, wavelength and velocity are affected.","revisionPoints":"Frequency of light remains constant during refraction.\nVelocity changes according to the optical medium.\nWavelength changes when velocity changes.\nRelation: v = fλ."},
{"no":105,"text":"When water is heated from 0°C to 10°C, its volume:","A":"Continuously increases","B":"Continuously decreases","C":"Remains constant","D":"First decreases and then increases","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Anomalous Expansion of Water","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Water behaves unusually between 0°C and 4°C.\nWhen heated from 0°C to 4°C, its volume decreases.\nAt 4°C, water has its maximum density.\nAbove 4°C, its volume starts increasing normally.\nHence, from 0°C to 10°C, volume first decreases and then increases.","revisionPoints":"Water has maximum density at approximately 4°C.\nThis is called the anomalous expansion of water.\nIt has important ecological significance for aquatic life in cold regions.\nIce is less dense than liquid water and therefore floats."},
{"no":106,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding a nuclear reactor:\nA self-sustained chain reaction is maintained in the reactor.\nThe fission process releases neutrons.\nThe released neutrons can cause further fission.\nEvery neutron produced in fission necessarily causes another fission.\nWhich of the above statements are correct according to the source?","A":"I and II","B":"I, II and III","C":"II and IV","D":"II, III and IV","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Energy","tags":["Nuclear Chain Reaction","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source identifies II, III and IV as the answer.\nNuclear fission releases neutrons.\nThese neutrons can initiate further fission reactions.\nA nuclear reactor is designed to sustain a controlled chain reaction.","revisionPoints":"Nuclear fission = splitting of a heavy nucleus.\nFission releases:\nEnergy\nNeutrons\nA chain reaction occurs when released neutrons cause further fission.\nImportant source note: Statement IV uses the absolute wording “every neutron”; this wording is physically questionable, but the source key gives option (d)."},
{"no":107,"text":"Match the following particles/entities with their characteristics:\nZero mass — Neutrino\nFractional charge — Quark\nFractional spin — Positron\nIntegral spin — Phonon\nWhich of the following is the correct combination according to the source?","A":"1, 2 and 4","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Particle Physics","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Neutrino is associated with extremely small/negligible rest mass in the source context.\nQuarks possess fractional electric charge.\nPhonons are associated with integral spin.\nThe source does not treat the positron as a fractional-spin particle.","revisionPoints":"Quarks have fractional electric charges such as +2/3 or −1/3.\nPositron = antiparticle of the electron.\nNeutrinos are electrically neutral.\nPhonon is a quantum description of lattice vibration."},
{"no":108,"text":"The Chandra X-ray telescope is named after:","A":"C. V. Raman","B":"Homi J. Bhabha","C":"Vikram Sarabhai","D":"Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["Chandra X-ray Observatory","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Chandra X-ray Observatory is named after astrophysicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar.\nHis work made major contributions to stellar structure and evolution.\nThe telescope observes astronomical objects in the X-ray region.","revisionPoints":"Chandra X-ray Observatory → X-ray astronomy.\nNamed after Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar.\nChandrasekhar is associated with the Chandrasekhar limit.\nX-ray astronomy helps study high-energy astronomical phenomena."},
{"no":109,"text":"If a person has a mass of 100 kg on Earth, what will be his mass on the Moon?","A":"16.6 kg","B":"50 kg","C":"100 kg","D":"600 kg","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Mass vs Weight","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Mass is the amount of matter in an object.\nIt does not change merely because the object moves to another celestial body.\nTherefore, the person's mass remains 100 kg.\nWeight changes because gravitational acceleration on the Moon is lower.","revisionPoints":"Mass remains constant.\nWeight = mass × gravitational acceleration.\nMoon's gravity is approximately one-sixth of Earth's.\nTherefore, an object's weight on the Moon is much lower, but its mass remains unchanged."},
{"no":110,"text":"Consider a simple pendulum. Which of the following statements are correct according to the source?\nThe acceleration of the bob is zero at the mean position.\nA given velocity is attained twice during an oscillation.\nBoth acceleration and velocity are zero at the extreme position.\nThe amplitude decreases with time.","A":"I and II","B":"II and III","C":"I and IV","D":"III and IV","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Simple Pendulum","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"At the mean position, the restoring force and corresponding acceleration are zero in the idealized simple-pendulum treatment.\nDuring an oscillation, a particular speed can generally be attained at two symmetric positions.\nAt the extreme position, velocity becomes zero, but acceleration is not zero.\nThe source key identifies I and II.","revisionPoints":"Mean position → maximum speed.\nExtreme position → zero instantaneous speed.\nRestoring acceleration is directed toward the mean position.\nIn a real pendulum, amplitude gradually decreases because of damping."},
{"no":111,"text":"Cloudy nights are generally warmer than clear nights because clouds:","A":"Absorb sunlight during the night","B":"Reflect back some of the heat radiated by the Earth","C":"Produce heat by condensation","D":"Increase atmospheric pressure","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Terrestrial Radiation","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"During the night, the Earth's surface emits long-wave terrestrial radiation.\nClouds absorb and re-radiate part of this outgoing radiation.\nThis reduces the loss of heat from the Earth's surface.\nTherefore, cloudy nights tend to remain warmer than clear nights.","revisionPoints":"Clouds influence the Earth's radiation balance.\nClear skies allow greater outgoing terrestrial radiation.\nCloud cover can reduce nocturnal cooling.\nThis is related to the greenhouse effect of atmospheric constituents."},
{"no":112,"text":"A sudden fall in the reading of a barometer generally indicates:","A":"Stormy weather","B":"Clear weather","C":"Dry weather","D":"No change in weather","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Barometric Pressure","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"A barometer measures atmospheric pressure.\nA rapid fall in pressure is commonly associated with an approaching low-pressure system.\nLow-pressure systems are often associated with cloud formation, strong winds and unsettled weather.\nHence, a sudden fall generally indicates stormy or unsettled weather.","revisionPoints":"Barometer → measures atmospheric pressure.\nFalling pressure → generally associated with worsening weather.\nRising pressure → generally associated with more stable conditions.\nCyclones are associated with low pressure at the centre."},
{"no":113,"text":"The half-life of a radioactive substance is 4 months. What will be the time required for three-fourths of the substance to decay?","A":"4 months","B":"6 months","C":"8 months","D":"12 months","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Three-fourths decayed means one-fourth remains.\nAfter one half-life:\nRemaining = 1/2.\nAfter two half-lives:\nRemaining = 1/4.\nTherefore, two half-lives are required.\n2 × 4 = 8 months.","revisionPoints":"Half-life = time required for radioactive quantity to reduce to half.\nAfter:\n1 half-life → 1/2 remains.\n2 half-lives → 1/4 remains.\n3 half-lives → 1/8 remains.\nRadioactive decay follows an exponential pattern."},
{"no":114,"text":"A town has a population of 5,00,000 in 1986. If its population increases by 13% and subsequently decreases by 6%, what will be its population?","A":"5,20,000","B":"5,31,100","C":"5,35,000","D":"5,65,000","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Initial population = 5,00,000.\nAfter 13% increase:\n5,00,000 × 1.13 = 5,65,000.\nThen population decreases by 6%:\n5,65,000 × 0.94 = 5,31,100.\nTherefore, final population = 5,31,100.","revisionPoints":"Successive percentage changes must be calculated sequentially.\nA 13% increase followed by a 6% decrease is not simply a 7% increase.\nFormula for successive changes:\nFinal value = Initial value × (1 + first change) × (1 + second change)."},
{"no":115,"text":"Which of the following graphs represents uniform motion?","A":"[Graph as given in source]","B":"[Graph as given in source]","C":"[Graph as given in source]","D":"[Graph as given in source]","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Kinematics","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The original figure/graph is not preserved in the extracted text.\nThe source identifies option (d) as the answer.\nIn uniform motion, an object covers equal distances in equal intervals of time.\nOn a distance-time graph, uniform motion is represented by a straight line with constant slope.","revisionPoints":"Uniform motion → constant velocity.\nDistance-time graph → straight line for uniform speed.\nSlope of a distance-time graph represents speed.\nA changing slope indicates changing speed."},
{"no":116,"text":"A chemical reaction generally becomes faster when the temperature is increased because:","A":"Molecular motion becomes more rapid","B":"Molecules become heavier","C":"The number of atoms decreases","D":"The reaction stops temporarily","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Reaction Rates","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Increasing temperature increases the kinetic energy of molecules.\nMolecules move more rapidly.\nMore molecules acquire sufficient energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier.\nTherefore, the number of effective collisions increases and the reaction rate generally increases.","revisionPoints":"Temperature affects reaction rate.\nHigher temperature → greater molecular kinetic energy.\nEffective collisions are important for chemical reactions.\nActivation energy is the minimum energy needed for an effective reaction."},
{"no":117,"text":"Which one of the following is not radioactive?","A":"Uranium","B":"Radium","C":"Thorium","D":"Zirconium","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Radioactivity","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Uranium, radium and thorium are naturally occurring radioactive elements.\nZirconium is not generally classified as a naturally radioactive element.\nTherefore, the correct answer is zirconium.","revisionPoints":"Uranium → radioactive.\nThorium → radioactive.\nRadium → radioactive.\nRadioactivity involves spontaneous nuclear transformation accompanied by radiation."},
{"no":118,"text":"Which of the following represents hydrocarbons in increasing order of molecular weight?","A":"Methane, Ethane, Propane, Butane","B":"Ethane, Methane, Butane, Propane","C":"Propane, Methane, Ethane, Butane","D":"Butane, Propane, Ethane, Methane","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Hydrocarbons","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Methane = CH₄\nEthane = C₂H₆\nPropane = C₃H₈\nButane = C₄H₁₀\nMolecular weight increases as the number of carbon atoms increases in this homologous series.","revisionPoints":"Alkanes follow the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nSuccessive members differ by CH₂.\nOrder of molecular weight:\nMethane < Ethane < Propane < Butane.\nMembers of a homologous series have similar chemical properties."},
{"no":119,"text":"The principle used for filling electrons in atomic orbitals in order of increasing energy is known as:","A":"Aufbau principle","B":"Pauli exclusion principle","C":"Heisenberg principle","D":"Hund's law","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Aufbau Principle","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The Aufbau principle states that electrons occupy orbitals in order of increasing energy.\nLower-energy orbitals are filled before higher-energy orbitals.\nThe Pauli principle limits the number and spin arrangement of electrons in an orbital.\nHund's rule governs electron distribution among degenerate orbitals.","revisionPoints":"Aufbau principle → order of orbital filling.\nPauli exclusion principle → no two electrons in an atom have the same set of four quantum numbers.\nHund's rule → degenerate orbitals are singly occupied before pairing.\nBasic filling order begins with 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p..."},
{"no":120,"text":"An aqueous solution of copper sulphate is acidic because:","A":"Copper sulphate is a strong acid","B":"Sulphate ions release hydrogen ions directly","C":"Copper sulphate undergoes hydrolysis in water","D":"Water becomes a strong acid in the presence of copper","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Salt Hydrolysis","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Copper sulphate dissolves in water and its ions interact with water molecules.\nThe hydrated copper ion undergoes hydrolysis and contributes to the acidic nature of the solution.\nTherefore, an aqueous solution of copper sulphate is acidic.","revisionPoints":"Hydrolysis involves reaction/interactions of ions with water.\nSalt solutions can be:\nAcidic\nBasic\nNeutral\nThe nature of a salt solution depends on the acid and base from which the salt is derived and on ion hydrolysis.\nCopper sulphate solution is commonly treated as acidic."},
{"no":121,"text":"Consider the following statements with reference to the Periodic Table of chemical elements:\nI. Ionisation potential gradually decreases along a period.II. In a group of elements, electron affinity decreases as the atomic weight increases.III. In a given period, electronegativity decreases as the atomic number increases.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"I and III","D":"II and III","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Periodic Table","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement I — Incorrect: Ionisation potential generally increases across a period.\nStatement II — Correct according to the source: Electron affinity generally shows a decreasing trend down a group.\nStatement III — Incorrect: Electronegativity generally increases across a period.","revisionPoints":"Across a period:\nAtomic radius generally decreases.\nIonisation energy generally increases.\nElectronegativity generally increases.\nDown a group:\nAtomic radius generally increases.\nIonisation energy generally decreases.\nPeriodic trends are governed mainly by effective nuclear charge and atomic size."},
{"no":122,"text":"Quartzite is metamorphosed from:","A":"Limestone","B":"Obsidian","C":"Sandstone","D":"Shale","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Metamorphic Rocks","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The supplied source gives option (a) as the answer.\nHowever, the standard geological classification is that quartzite forms from sandstone under metamorphism.\nTherefore, this question contains an apparent source/key error.","revisionPoints":"Quartzite → Sandstone.\nMarble → Limestone.\nMetamorphic rocks form when existing rocks undergo heat, pressure or chemically active fluids.\nExamples:\nSandstone → Quartzite\nLimestone → Marble\nShale → Slate"},
{"no":123,"text":"Which of the following cell organelles play the most significant role in protein synthesis?","A":"Lysosome and Centrosome","B":"Endoplasmic reticulum and Ribosome","C":"Golgi apparatus and Mitochondria","D":"Lysosome and Mitochondria","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Protein Synthesis","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Ribosomes are the primary sites of protein synthesis.\nRibosomes attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum synthesize proteins destined for secretion or membranes.\nTherefore, the source identifies the endoplasmic reticulum and ribosome.","revisionPoints":"Ribosome → protein synthesis.\nRough ER → contains ribosomes.\nGolgi apparatus → modification, sorting and packaging.\nLysosome → intracellular digestion.\nMitochondria → major site of aerobic energy production."},
{"no":124,"text":"Assertion (A): Scientists can cut apart and paste together DNA molecules at will, regardless of the source of the molecules.\nReason (R): DNA fragments can be manipulated using restriction endonucleases and DNA ligases.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT a correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Recombinant DNA Technology","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Assertion: The source treats the ability to manipulate DNA from different sources as true.\nReason: Restriction enzymes can cut DNA at specific sites.\nDNA ligase can join DNA fragments.\nThe source key states that the reason does not constitute the complete explanation of the assertion.","revisionPoints":"Restriction endonucleases → cut DNA at specific recognition sites.\nDNA ligase → joins DNA fragments.\nThese are important tools in recombinant DNA technology.\nGenetic engineering involves cutting, joining and introducing selected DNA sequences."},
{"no":125,"text":"The cellular and molecular control of programmed cell death is known as:","A":"Apoptosis","B":"Ageing","C":"Degeneration","D":"Necrosis","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Apoptosis","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Apoptosis is programmed cell death.\nIt is a regulated cellular process.\nIt helps remove unwanted or damaged cells in a controlled manner.","revisionPoints":"Apoptosis → programmed cell death.\nNecrosis → generally associated with uncontrolled cell death due to injury or pathological conditions.\nApoptosis is important in:\nDevelopment\nTissue maintenance\nRemoval of damaged cells."},
{"no":126,"text":"Which organelle in the cell, other than nucleus, contains DNA?","A":"Centriole","B":"Golgi apparatus","C":"Lysosome","D":"Mitochondrion","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Mitochondrial DNA","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Mitochondria contain their own DNA.\nThey are therefore described as semi-autonomous organelles.\nMost cellular DNA is located in the nucleus, but mitochondrial DNA is separate.","revisionPoints":"Mitochondria → contain mtDNA.\nMitochondria are associated with cellular energy production.\nMitochondria are often called the powerhouses of the cell.\nMitochondrial inheritance is generally associated with the maternal line."},
{"no":127,"text":"Epiphytes are plants which depend on other plants for:","A":"Food","B":"Mechanical support","C":"Shade","D":"Water","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Botany","tags":["Epiphytes","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Epiphytes grow on other plants, particularly trees.\nThey use the host plant primarily as a physical support.\nThey do not necessarily obtain their food directly from the host.\nMany epiphytes obtain water and nutrients from the surrounding environment.","revisionPoints":"Epiphyte = plant growing on another plant for physical support.\nEpiphytes are not necessarily parasites.\nExamples include many:\nOrchids\nFerns\nMosses."},
{"no":128,"text":"Antigen is a substance which:","A":"Destroys harmful bacteria","B":"Is used to treat poisoning","C":"Lowers body temperature","D":"Stimulates formation of antibody","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Immunology","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"An antigen is a substance recognized by the immune system.\nIt can stimulate an immune response, including antibody production.\nAntibodies are produced by B cells/plasma cells in response to appropriate antigens.","revisionPoints":"Antigen → triggers or participates in an immune response.\nAntibody → immune protein that specifically recognizes an antigen.\nAntigens may include proteins, polysaccharides and other molecules.\nAntigen-antibody interaction is important in immunity and diagnostics."},
{"no":129,"text":"Which feature of DNA makes it uniquely suited to store and transmit genetic information from generation to generation?","A":"Complementarity of the two strands","B":"Double helix","C":"Number of base pairs per turn","D":"Sugar-phosphate backbone","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["DNA Structure","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The two DNA strands have complementary bases:\nA pairs with T.\nG pairs with C.\nEach strand can therefore serve as a template for formation of the complementary strand.\nThis allows accurate replication and transmission of genetic information.","revisionPoints":"DNA has a double-stranded structure.\nBase pairing:\nA–T\nG–C\nComplementarity is fundamental to DNA replication.\nSugar-phosphate backbone provides structural stability."},
{"no":130,"text":"An insect-resistant cotton variety was developed by transferring a toxin gene from which bacterium?","A":"Bacillus subtilis","B":"Bacillus thuringiensis","C":"Bacillus amyloliquefaciens","D":"Bacillus globlii","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Bt Cotton","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Bt cotton contains a gene derived from Bacillus thuringiensis.\nThe bacterium produces insecticidal Bt proteins/toxins.\nThese proteins provide resistance against certain insect pests.","revisionPoints":"Bt cotton → Bacillus thuringiensis.\nBt toxin is an example of a biological insect-control mechanism.\nBt crops are genetically modified crops.\nThe inserted gene enables the plant to produce insecticidal protein."},
{"no":131,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nList I – Bone\nList II – Name\nI. Breastbone\nA. Clavicle\nII. Collarbone\nB. Patella\nIII. Kneecap\nC. Scapula\nIV. Shoulder blade\nD. Sternum\nCodes:","A":"I-D, II-A, III-C, IV-B","B":"I-A, II-D, III-C, IV-B","C":"I-A, II-D, III-B, IV-C","D":"I-D, II-A, III-B, IV-C","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Human Skeleton","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Breastbone → Sternum.\nCollarbone → Clavicle.\nKneecap → Patella.\nShoulder blade → Scapula.\nTherefore, the correct combination is D-A-B-C.","revisionPoints":"Sternum → breastbone.\nClavicle → collarbone.\nPatella → kneecap.\nScapula → shoulder blade.\nFemur → thigh bone.\nTibia → major shin bone."},
{"no":132,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Tapeworm is a hermaphrodite.II. Roundworm has separate sexes.III. Filaria is caused by a nematode.IV. Guinea worm is an annelid.\nWhich of these are correct?","A":"I and II","B":"I, II and III","C":"III and IV","D":"II, III and IV","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Parasitic Worms","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Statement I — Correct: Tapeworms are generally hermaphroditic.\nStatement II — Correct: Male and female roundworms are separate.\nStatement III — Correct: Filarial worms belong to the nematodes.\nStatement IV — Incorrect: Guinea worm is a nematode, not an annelid.","revisionPoints":"Tapeworm → Platyhelminthes.\nRoundworm → Nematoda.\nFilarial worms → Nematoda.\nGuinea worm (Dracunculus medinensis) → nematode.\nAnnelids include organisms such as earthworms and leeches."},
{"no":133,"text":"Match the achievements in genetics with the scientists:\nI. Discovery of transduction and conjugation in bacteriaII. Establishing sex-linked inheritanceIII. Isolation of DNA polymerase from E. coliIV. Establishing the complete genetic code\nScientists:\nA. KhuranaB. KornbergC. LederbergD. MorganE. Ochoa","A":"I-D, II-C, III-B, IV-A","B":"I-C, II-D, III-A, IV-E","C":"I-D, II-C, III-A, IV-E","D":"I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-A","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Genetics History","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Transduction and conjugation → Joshua Lederberg.\nSex-linked inheritance → Thomas Hunt Morgan.\nDNA polymerase → Arthur Kornberg.\nComplete genetic code → Har Gobind Khorana.\nTherefore, the correct sequence is C-D-B-A.","revisionPoints":"Lederberg → bacterial genetics.\nMorgan → chromosome theory and sex-linked inheritance.\nKornberg → DNA polymerase.\nKhorana → work on deciphering the genetic code.\nGenetics questions often combine scientist + discovery."},
{"no":134,"text":"“Athlete’s Foot” is a disease caused by:","A":"Bacteria","B":"Fungus","C":"Protozoan","D":"Nematode","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Health & Diseases","tags":["Athlete's Foot","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Athlete's foot is a fungal infection of the skin.\nIt commonly affects areas between the toes.\nIt belongs to a group of fungal skin infections called dermatophytoses.","revisionPoints":"Athlete's foot → fungal infection.\nRingworm → fungal infection.\nFungal infections commonly affect skin, hair and nails.\nMoist environments can favour fungal growth."},
{"no":135,"text":"In eye donation, which part of the eye is transplanted from the donor?","A":"Cornea","B":"Lens","C":"Retina","D":"The whole eye","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Eye Donation","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The cornea is the transparent front part of the eye.\nCorneal tissue can be transplanted to restore vision in suitable patients.\nThe entire eye is not transplanted as a functional replacement.","revisionPoints":"Cornea → transparent front surface of eye.\nLens → focuses light onto the retina.\nRetina → contains photoreceptor cells.\nCorneal transplantation is also called keratoplasty."},
{"no":136,"text":"A man whose blood group is not known meets with a serious accident and needs blood transfusion immediately. Which blood group mentioned below and readily available in the hospital will be safe for transfusion?","A":"O, Rh−","B":"O, Rh+","C":"AB, Rh−","D":"AB, Rh+","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Blood Groups","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source gives O, Rh+ as the answer.\nIn the ABO system, type O red cells lack A and B antigens.\nRh compatibility also matters during transfusion.\nThe exact emergency choice depends on the blood component and clinical circumstances.","revisionPoints":"O red cells → lack A and B surface antigens.\nAB individuals have both A and B antigens.\nRh factor is an important blood-group antigen.\nIn modern emergency transfusion practice, O-negative red cells are commonly preferred when the patient's blood group is unknown, where available and appropriate."},
{"no":137,"text":"“Metastasis” is the process by which:","A":"Cells divide rapidly under the influence of drugs","B":"Cancer cells spread through the blood or lymphatic system to other sites or organs","C":"Chromosomes attach to the spindle before moving to anaphase poles","D":"Cancer cells are successfully inhibited from dividing further","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Cancer Biology","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source explicitly gives (a) as its answer.\nHowever, the definition in option (b) is the standard meaning of metastasis.\nMetastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from the original tumour to distant tissues or organs through blood or lymphatic pathways.\nTherefore, this is a clear source-answer error.","revisionPoints":"Metastasis → spread of malignant cancer cells to distant sites.\nRoutes include:\nBloodstream\nLymphatic system\nPrimary tumour → original site.\nSecondary/metastatic tumour → new site formed after spread."},
{"no":138,"text":"Match the following substances with their physiological roles:\nSubstance\nPhysiological role\nI. Ptyalin\nA. Converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin\nII. Pepsin\nB. Digests starch\nIII. Renin\nC. Digests proteins\nIV. Oxytocin\nD. Hydrolyses fats\nE. Induces contraction of smooth muscles\nCodes:","A":"I-B, II-C, III-A, IV-E","B":"I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-E","C":"I-B, II-C, III-E, IV-A","D":"I-C, II-A, III-B, IV-D","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Digestive Enzymes","Hormones","UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Ptyalin → digests starch.\nPepsin → digests proteins.\nRenin → involved in conversion of angiotensinogen into angiotensin.\nOxytocin → induces smooth muscle contraction.","revisionPoints":"Ptyalin = salivary amylase.\nPepsin → protein digestion in stomach.\nOxytocin → uterine contraction and milk ejection.\nRenin is part of the renin-angiotensin system."},
{"no":139,"text":"If \\(x^2 + y^2 = 34\\), determine the values asked for in the question.","A":"±5, ±3","B":"±3, ±5","C":"±4, ±5","D":"±2, ±5","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The extracted source has garbled mathematical notation for this question.\nIt gives the answer as (b).\nFrom the clearly preserved relation \\(x^2+y^2=34\\), the pair 5 and 3 satisfies the equation:\n\\(5^2 + 3^2 = 25 + 9 = 34\\).\nThe exact requested expression is not completely recoverable from the source text.","revisionPoints":"When \\(x^2+y^2=34\\), integer possibilities include:\n\\(x = ±5\\)\n\\(y = ±3\\)\nor vice versa.\nSquaring removes the sign, so both positive and negative values can satisfy the equation.\nAlways check the original equation after obtaining candidate values."},
{"no":140,"text":"A worker reaches his factory 3 minutes late if his speed from his house to the factory is 5 km/hr. If he walks at a speed of 6 km/hr, he reaches the factory 7 minutes early. The distance of the factory from his house is:","A":"4 km","B":"5 km","C":"6 km","D":"7 km","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Difference between the two travel times:\n3 minutes late + 7 minutes early = 10 minutes.\nLet distance = \\(D\\) km.\nDifference in travel time:\\[ \\frac{D}{5}-\\frac{D}{6}=\\frac{10}{60} \\]\nTherefore:\\[ \\frac{D}{30}=\\frac{1}{6} \\]\\[ D=5\\text{ km} \\]\nHence, distance = 5 km.","revisionPoints":"Convert minutes into hours when speed is in km/h.\nTime = Distance ÷ Speed.\nDifference between early and late arrival gives the difference in travel times.\nSuch questions are efficiently solved using one variable for distance."},
{"no":141,"text":"A conveyor belt delivers baggage at the rate of 3 tons in 5 minutes, and a second conveyor belt delivers baggage at the rate of 1 ton in 2 minutes. How much time will it take to get 33 tons of baggage delivered using both the conveyor belts?","A":"25 minutes 30 seconds","B":"30 minutes","C":"35 minutes","D":"40 minutes 45 seconds","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"First conveyor rate:\n3 tons / 5 minutes = 0.6 ton/minute.\nSecond conveyor rate:\n1 ton / 2 minutes = 0.5 ton/minute.\nCombined rate:\n0.6 + 0.5 = 1.1 tons/minute.\nTime required:\n33 ÷ 1.1 = 30 minutes.","revisionPoints":"When two machines work simultaneously, add their rates.\nRate = Work ÷ Time.\nTime = Work ÷ Combined rate.\nBe careful to keep all rates in the same units."},
{"no":142,"text":"Water is filled in a container in such a manner that its volume doubles after every five minutes. It takes 30 minutes for the container to be full. In how much time will it be one-fourth full?","A":"7 minutes 30 seconds","B":"10 minutes","C":"20 minutes","D":"25 minutes","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The source states that the volume doubles every 5 minutes.\nIf the container is full at 30 minutes:\n25 minutes → 1/2 full.\n20 minutes → 1/4 full.\nThus, mathematically, the container should be one-fourth full at 20 minutes.\nThe supplied source, however, gives (a) 7 minutes 30 seconds.\nTherefore, the source answer appears inconsistent with the stated doubling condition.","revisionPoints":"In a doubling process, work backward by halving the quantity.\nIf full at 30 minutes:\n25 min → 1/2\n20 min → 1/4\n15 min → 1/8\nThis is a classic exponential growth problem.\nSource key should be treated cautiously here."},
{"no":143,"text":"A city has a population of 3,00,000 out of which 1,80,000 are males. 50% of the population is literate. If 70% of the males are literate, the number of literate females is:","A":"24,000","B":"30,000","C":"54,000","D":"60,000","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Total population = 3,00,000.\nTotal literates = 50% × 3,00,000 = 1,50,000.\nLiterate males = 70% × 1,80,000 = 1,26,000.\nLiterate females:\n1,50,000 − 1,26,000\n= 24,000.","revisionPoints":"Literacy percentage applies to the total population unless otherwise specified.\nFirst calculate total literates.\nThen subtract the known literate population.\nAlways distinguish between total population and male/female population."},
{"no":144,"text":"In a survey, 80% owned a car, 60% owned a mobile phone and 55% owned both. What percentage owned a car or a mobile phone or both?","A":"65%","B":"80%","C":"85%","D":"97.5%","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Use the inclusion-exclusion formula:\nCar or mobile = Car + Mobile − Both.\nTherefore:\n80 + 60 − 55 = 85%.","revisionPoints":"Formula:\nn(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B).\n“Both” is subtracted because it is counted twice.\n“Or” in such questions generally includes both."},
{"no":145,"text":"In 1930, a person’s age was 8 times that of his son. In 1938, the father’s age became ten times that of his son’s age in 1930. The ages of the son and father in 1940 were, respectively:","A":"16 years, 58 years","B":"15 years, 50 years","C":"14 years, 42 years","D":"13 years, 34 years","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"Let son's age in 1930 = x.\nFather's age = 8x.\nIn 1938, father's age = 8x + 8.\nAccording to the question:\n8x + 8 = 10x.\nTherefore:\n2x = 8\nx = 4.\nIn 1940:\nSon = 4 + 10 = 14 years.\nFather = 32 + 10 = 42 years.","revisionPoints":"Age difference between two people remains constant.\nUse algebra when multiple years are involved.\nConvert all ages to the same reference year before comparing them."},
{"no":146,"text":"A circle of 1 m radius is drawn inside a square as shown in the figure. What is the area of the shaded portion in m²?","A":"4π","B":"1 + π/2","C":"1/4(π/4)","D":"1 − π/4 (source formatting is distorted)","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The original figure is not preserved in the extracted text.\nThe mathematical notation of the options is also distorted.\nThe source gives (d) as the answer.\nTherefore, the exact shaded-area calculation cannot be reconstructed reliably from the text alone.","revisionPoints":"Circle area:\nA = πr².\nFor a shaded-region problem:\nIdentify the total area.\nIdentify the unshaded area.\nSubtract one from the other as required.\nAlways refer to the figure when the geometry depends on a specific shaded region."},
{"no":147,"text":"Most guitarists are bearded males. If A represents all males, B represents bearded males and C represents all male guitarists, then the correct diagram for their relation (shaded portion) is:","A":"Diagram given in source","B":"Diagram given in source","C":"Diagram given in source","D":"Diagram given in source","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"A represents all males.\nB represents bearded males.\nC represents male guitarists.\nTherefore, C must be within A because male guitarists are males.\nThere is an overlap between C and B because most guitarists are stated to be bearded.\nThe source identifies diagram (a).","revisionPoints":"“All males” represents the largest relevant set.\nMale guitarists form a subset of males.\n“Most” indicates an overlap but not necessarily complete inclusion.\nVenn diagrams are useful for analysing set relationships."},
{"no":148,"text":"In the above figure, ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral, AB = BC and ∠BAC = 70°, then ∠ADC is:","A":"40°","B":"80°","C":"110°","D":"140°","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"AB = BC.\nTherefore, triangle ABC is isosceles.\nHence:\n∠BAC = ∠ACB = 70°.\nTherefore:\n∠ABC = 180° − 70° − 70°\n= 40°.\nABCD is cyclic.\nOpposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary:\n∠ABC + ∠ADC = 180°.\nTherefore:\n∠ADC = 180° − 40°\n= 140°.","revisionPoints":"Equal sides → equal opposite angles.\nSum of angles in a triangle = 180°.\nOpposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral = supplementary.\nThese two geometric properties can be combined to solve the question."},
{"no":149,"text":"“Anand must be a vegetarian because he is a Buddhist.” The argument assumes that:","A":"Most Buddhists are vegetarians","B":"All Buddhists are vegetarians","C":"Only Buddhists are vegetarians","D":"Most vegetarians are Buddhists","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"The argument connects being Buddhist with being vegetarian.\nThe unstated assumption is that vegetarianism is sufficiently common among Buddhists to support the conclusion.\nThe source identifies “most Buddhists are vegetarians” as the required assumption.\n“All Buddhists” would be a stronger universal claim.","revisionPoints":"An assumption is an unstated premise in an argument.\n“Most” means a majority, not all.\n“All” is a universal statement.\n“Only” establishes a restrictive relationship."},
{"no":150,"text":"A person travels from X to Y at a speed of 40 kmph and returns by increasing his speed by 50%. What is his average speed for both trips?","A":"36 kmph","B":"45 kmph","C":"48 kmph","D":"50 kmph","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2001"],"expl":"First speed = 40 km/h.\n50% increase:\n40 + 20 = 60 km/h.\nThe distances in both directions are equal.\nAverage speed for equal distances:\\[ \\frac{2uv}{u+v} \\]\nTherefore:\\[ \\frac{2(40)(60)}{40+60}=48 \\]\nAverage speed = 48 km/h.","revisionPoints":"Average speed = Total distance ÷ Total time.\nFor equal distances:\nAverage speed = 2uv/(u+v).\nDo not use the simple arithmetic average unless the time intervals are equal.\nEqual-distance problems generally require the harmonic-mean approach."},
];
